UNITS.append([
    ("Individual Action on Climate Change", "Discuss personal responsibility for reducing carbon emissions.", "有人认为应对气候变化主要是政府和企业的责任，个人努力作用有限。请谈谈你的看法。", "Climate change often feels too vast for individual effort, yet personal choices still matter in slowing it. On the one hand, governments and industries clearly control the largest emissions and must lead through regulation and investment. Moreover, systemic change can transform whole sectors far faster than millions of small decisions. Only governments can set standards for power stations, and only large firms can redesign a supply chain. However, dismissing ordinary behaviour is both inaccurate and dangerous, because consumption patterns shape market demand. Consequently, when households reduce energy use, waste less food and travel more efficiently, businesses eventually respond. On the other hand, guilt-driven campaigns can overwhelm people and lead to apathy rather than action. A constructive approach focuses on achievable, visible steps such as insulating homes, choosing public transport and eating less meat. Communities also amplify impact by sharing skills and pressing local councils. Ordinary choices matter most when they become ordinary expectations. In conclusion, individual action cannot replace policy, but it creates the public will that makes policy possible.", "Governments and industries must lead on emissions, yet personal choices shape demand and cannot be dismissed. Guilt, however, breeds apathy, so achievable steps like insulating homes, using public transport and eating less meat work best. Communities amplify change. Individual action supports, rather than replaces, effective policy. Large emitters obviously carry the greater duty, and regulation can change an entire industry at once. Yet demand influences what companies dare to offer, and visible habits spread through neighbourhoods and schools. Guilt-based messaging tends to produce resignation, so practical steps and community action are more useful than perfection.", "Ask whether one person's recycling truly matters and the honest answer is complicated. Clearly, a single household cannot compensate for a coal-fired power station, and campaigners sometimes exaggerate the power of lifestyle changes. Nevertheless, habits spread socially, and visible choices influence neighbours, schools and workplaces. Furthermore, consumers collectively determine which products succeed, so demand for durable goods sends a powerful signal. I would argue that the aim is not personal purity but political pressure: people who change their own routines are more credible when they request better infrastructure. Practical priorities include cutting food waste, using less heating and avoiding short flights. At the same time, voters must hold leaders accountable for bold energy policies. None of this requires a heroic lifestyle; consistency matters more than purity, and shared expectations make each step easier to maintain. In short, everyday action and systemic reform reinforce each other rather than competing for attention.", "emissions systemic consumption apathy amplify"),
    ("Reducing Single-Use Plastics", "Examine solutions to plastic pollution and disposable culture.", "一次性塑料制品造成的污染日益严重。请分析问题的成因，并提出可行的解决办法。", "Single-use plastics have become a defining symbol of modern pollution, and the scale of the problem can seem overwhelming. On the one hand, plastic packaging is cheap, light and hygienic, which explains its dominance in food delivery and medicine. Moreover, it reduces breakage and extends shelf life, preventing considerable waste. Hospitals and food banks also rely on it for safety, so careless slogans can mislead. However, these brief conveniences leave materials that persist for centuries and harm marine life. Consequently, rivers carry tonnes of debris into the ocean each year, and microplastics now appear even in drinking water. On the other hand, simply blaming consumers ignores the producers who design products with no realistic reuse option. Effective solutions must therefore act at several levels: governments can ban avoidable items and require deposit-return schemes, while companies should redesign packaging. Individuals contribute by carrying reusable bottles and refusing unnecessary extras. Clearer labels would help shoppers choose materials that can actually be processed locally. In conclusion, tackling plastic waste demands shared responsibility rather than token gestures.", "Plastic is cheap, light and hygienic, but disposable packaging persists for centuries, harms marine life and enters drinking water. Consumers alone cannot solve it: bans, deposit-return schemes and redesigned packaging matter more. Reusable bottles and refusing extras also help. Real progress needs shared responsibility. Packaging protects goods and prevents spoilage, which is why it became so common. The failure lies in designing items for a single use when they could be refilled or repaired. Bans on avoidable products, deposit schemes and better labelling address the supply side, while reusable bottles help at household level.", "A sandwich wrapped in layers of plastic illustrates the absurdity of our throwaway culture. Admittedly, the material protects food and keeps medical supplies sterile, so a complete ban would create new problems. The real issue is duration: an item used for ten minutes may survive for five hundred years. Wildlife mistakes fragments for food, and microplastics accumulate through the food chain. I believe policy should target the worst offenders first, such as unnecessary cutlery, straws and oversized packaging. Rechargeable deposit systems have worked well in several countries and could be extended widely. Retailers also influence behaviour by offering refills and charging for bags. Education helps, but convenience usually wins unless sustainable options are equally easy. Consumers also deserve clear instructions, because uncertainty makes people give up. When the convenient option is also the responsible one, far fewer lectures are needed. Ultimately, reducing plastic is a design challenge as much as a moral one.", "hygienic debris microplastics deposit redesign"),
    ("The Shift to Renewable Energy", "Evaluate the transition from fossil fuels to clean power.", "许多国家正在大力发展可再生能源。请分析这一转变的意义和面临的困难，并提出建议。", "Renewable energy is expanding rapidly as countries seek to reduce emissions and dependence on imported fuels. On the one hand, solar and wind power produce electricity without greenhouse gases, and their costs have fallen dramatically. Moreover, local generation can strengthen energy security and create skilled jobs in manufacturing and maintenance. Rooftop panels and community wind projects let towns keep more of the money they spend on power. However, sunlight and wind are intermittent, so grids need storage, better connections and careful management. Consequently, transition periods can bring price instability and complaints from communities affected by new infrastructure. On the other hand, delaying action would lock in expensive fossil fuel plants for decades. Governments should therefore combine investment in grids and batteries with clear, stable policies that encourage private capital. They must also support workers leaving coal regions through retraining. Public consultation helps, since local opposition often grows when benefits are explained too late. In conclusion, the shift to clean energy is technically achievable and economically sensible, provided its social costs are managed fairly.", "Solar and wind power cut emissions, improve security and create jobs, but their intermittency demands storage and stronger grids. Delaying the transition locks in fossil fuel dependence. Stable policies, grid investment and retraining for coal workers make the change fair and affordable. Falling costs make clean power competitive, and domestic generation reduces reliance on imported fuel. The obstacles are practical: wind and sun are unpredictable, so storage and transmission lines must expand together. Stable rules attract investment, while retraining and shared savings ease the burden on coal communities.", "Solar panels and wind turbines are no longer experimental; in many regions they are now the cheapest source of new electricity. This change matters because energy production drives a large share of global emissions. Supporters also emphasise independence from volatile fuel markets, which protects households from sudden price rises. Critics, however, rightly point out that the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow. Without adequate storage, backup plants remain necessary, and new transmission lines often meet local resistance. I would add that the transition must be just: communities built around coal cannot simply be abandoned. Governments should plan early, invest in training and share the benefits through lower bills. Clear long-term rules are essential, since uncertainty discourages investment. Delaying construction does not remove these costs; it merely passes a larger bill to the next generation. Overall, the barriers are largely organisational rather than scientific.", "intermittent grids emissions security transition"),
    ("Sustainable Urban Transport", "Discuss greener commuting and city traffic solutions.", "城市交通拥堵和尾气污染让居民困扰。请分析原因，并提出建设更环保交通系统的建议。", "Traffic congestion and exhaust fumes make many cities unpleasant places to live, but transport systems can be redesigned. On the one hand, private cars offer unmatched flexibility and are often essential for families in poorly connected suburbs. Moreover, drivers and freight companies depend on roads for commerce. In rural areas, a car may be the only realistic way to reach work or hospital. However, millions of individual journeys create gridlock, poor air quality and noisy streets. Consequently, residents suffer respiratory problems and lose hours each week sitting in traffic. On the other hand, simply building more roads rarely solves the problem, because extra capacity quickly attracts more vehicles. A better strategy prioritises reliable public transport, safe cycling lanes and walkable neighbourhoods. Cities might also charge drivers to enter busy centres and invest the revenue in buses and trains. Better information about arrivals would make public transport feel less risky. In conclusion, greener mobility is achievable when planners make sustainable choices convenient and affordable rather than merely moral.", "Cars provide flexibility, yet mass car use causes congestion, air pollution and lost time, and new roads only attract more traffic. Cities should improve buses and trains, build safe cycle lanes and charge for city-centre driving. Sustainable transport succeeds when it is convenient and affordable. Cars give freedom, especially where buses are rare, and businesses need deliveries. However, widespread driving fills streets, pollutes air and wastes time, while new lanes simply attract more vehicles. Frequent services, protected cycle lanes and congestion pricing can shift trips, provided the money returns to better transport.", "Every morning, the same streets fill with drivers who would rather be somewhere else. Cars are convenient, particularly for parents and workers living far from stations, and businesses rely on deliveries. Yet a city designed around vehicles leaves little room for children, trees or conversation, and stagnant traffic damages health. Experience shows that expanding roads postpones rather than solves congestion. The more promising path is to make alternatives genuinely attractive: frequent buses, integrated ticketing, protected bike lanes and shorter distances between homes and services. Congestion charges, if introduced carefully, can fund these improvements and reduce the number of unnecessary trips. Officials should consult residents, since measures perceived as punishments quickly lose support. Fairness also matters, since charges hurt rural drivers least when revenue improves the buses they might use. Small pilots allow cities to test what works before committing. In the end, people choose greener transport when it respects their time.", "congestion exhaust gridlock sustainable affordable"),
    ("Green Spaces in Cities", "Argue for parks and trees in urban planning.", "城市建设中应当保留足够的公园和绿地。请说明原因，并谈谈如何平衡土地使用的需求。", "As cities grow denser, parks and trees are often squeezed out by housing and roads, yet they are essential to urban life. On the one hand, green spaces improve physical and mental health by offering places to exercise, rest and escape noise. Moreover, vegetation cools streets, absorbs rainwater and supports birds and insects. A shaded playground can remain usable during heat that makes paved areas unbearable. Consequently, neighbourhoods with trees are more pleasant and better equipped to handle heatwaves. On the other hand, land is scarce and expensive, and families need affordable homes. Planners therefore face a genuine trade-off rather than a simple choice. A creative response is to integrate nature into development: rooftop gardens, pocket parks on vacant lots, and tree-lined walking routes require little space. Authorities can also protect existing parks through legal designation before land values rise further. Maintenance budgets matter as much as the original promise to plant trees. In conclusion, green infrastructure should be treated as basic urban equipment, not decoration added only when budgets allow.", "Parks and trees improve health, cool streets and manage rainwater, but cities also need scarce land for housing. Rather than choosing one priority, planners can add rooftop gardens, pocket parks and tree-lined routes while legally protecting existing parks. Green space is essential infrastructure. Green areas lower temperatures, absorb rain and give residents somewhere to exercise and meet, but housing is genuinely scarce. Planners do not have to choose: small pocket parks, rooftop gardens and tree-lined routes fit into tight sites. Legal protection and proper maintenance ensure these spaces survive future budgets.", "Urban residents often discover how much they value a nearby park only when it disappears. Green areas give children room to play, offer relief from concrete and heat, and bring different neighbours together. Trees also filter polluted air and slow storm water, reducing damage after heavy rain. Nevertheless, cities cannot ignore the shortage of decent housing, and large parks consume land that could hold hundreds of homes. My preferred solution is to think vertically and locally: gardens on roofs, small community plots and vegetation along pavements all deliver benefits without huge sites. Where major parks already exist, they should be protected permanently and maintained properly. Planning decisions must include residents, since a space people use will be defended. The smallest sites are often the most used, precisely because they are close to home. Ultimately, a city that makes room for nature is not wasting land but investing in wellbeing.", "denser vegetation heatwaves integrate designation"),
    ("Responsibility to Future Generations", "Explore ethical duties toward people not yet born.", "我们今天的消费和发展方式会影响后代的生活。请讨论当代人对未来世代的责任。", "Decisions made today will shape the world inherited by generations who cannot yet speak for themselves. On the one hand, it is tempting to prioritise immediate needs such as jobs, housing and affordable energy, especially when voters demand results now. Moreover, predicting the precise needs of future societies is genuinely difficult. Technology may solve problems we cannot yet imagine, so humility is justified. However, uncertainty is not an excuse for carelessness, because some consequences are already clear: rising seas, lost species and depleted resources will not repair themselves quickly. Consequently, present-day consumption effectively borrows from people who have no choice in the matter. On the other hand, excessive sacrifice today could also harm the poor, who deserve decent lives now. The fairest approach applies the precautionary principle to irreversible damage while investing in education and technology that expand future options. Restoring forests and cutting emissions today leaves later generations more freedom, not less. In conclusion, stewardship is a duty of fairness: we should leave behind possibilities, not merely problems.", "Present choices affect generations unable to represent themselves. Immediate needs matter, but rising seas, lost species and depleted resources are predictable harms, so uncertainty is no excuse. Fairness demands precaution on irreversible damage plus investment in education and technology. We should leave possibilities, not problems. Nobody can know exactly what tomorrow's citizens will need, and current poverty deserves attention too. Yet some harms are already visible and difficult to undo, from rising seas to disappearing species. Precaution on irreversible damage, combined with education and research, preserves options and treats the unborn as members of our community.", "Few political systems give a voice to people who have not been born, which helps explain why long-term environmental damage is often postponed. Yet our obligations to them are real. We already know that carbon emissions accumulate, that forests take centuries to recover and that waste outlasts institutions. Ignoring such facts is not neutral; it transfers costs forward. At the same time, asking today's poor to accept hardship for hypothetical descendants would be unjust. The balance lies in acting where evidence is strong and avoiding actions that cannot be reversed. Protecting ecosystems, stabilising the climate and funding research are sensible investments rather than sacrifices. Young people themselves increasingly demand this, suggesting the concern is not abstract. Their voices may be absent today, but our choices will speak for us. To conclude, responsibility to the unborn is best expressed by preserving choices and repairing the damage we can already see.", "inherited depleted precautionary stewardship irreversible"),
    ("Fast Fashion and Sustainability", "Examine clothing consumption and its environmental cost.", "快时尚让衣服便宜又多样，却带来浪费。请分析其影响，并提出更可持续的穿衣方式。", "Cheap, trend-driven clothing has transformed wardrobes, but its environmental and human costs are increasingly hard to ignore. On the one hand, fast fashion makes style accessible and allows people to express themselves without spending much. Moreover, the industry provides employment in many manufacturing regions. For millions of workers, those factories offer a route out of poverty. However, garments are often designed to be discarded after a few washes, and synthetic fabrics shed microfibres into waterways. Consequently, mountains of textile waste accumulate while workers face low wages and unsafe conditions. On the other hand, telling consumers simply to buy less can sound unrealistic when families need variety on a budget. Practical alternatives include choosing durable pieces, repairing damaged items and buying second-hand. Governments can also require producers to take responsibility for textile waste. Clearer labelling would tell buyers which fabrics last and which can be recycled. Repair services and second-hand markets make durability practical rather than noble. In conclusion, a sustainable wardrobe depends less on guilt than on better design, fairer rules and habits that value longevity.", "Fast fashion offers affordable style and jobs, but short-lived garments create textile waste, microfibre pollution and poor working conditions. Solutions include durable clothing, repairs and second-hand shopping, plus producer responsibility laws. Sustainability depends on design and fair rules, not guilt alone. Low prices help families, and garment factories provide jobs, but disposable clothes waste water, shed fibres and exploit workers. Longer-lasting designs, repairs and second-hand shopping reduce the damage without demanding sacrifice. Producer responsibility laws and clear labels shift the burden upstream, where design decisions are actually made.", "A shirt can now cost less than a coffee, which encourages people to treat clothing as disposable. Admittedly, this affordability helps low-income families dress decently and follow changing trends. The problem is the hidden chain behind the price tag: intensive water use, chemical dyes and overworked factory employees. Synthetic blends are particularly difficult to recycle, so discarded uniforms and jackets end up in landfill or are shipped abroad. I believe change must involve both producers and buyers. Brands should publish supply-chain information and design garments that can be repaired. Consumers can extend the life of clothes through careful washing and swapping with friends. Schools could teach basic sewing again. None of this demands expensive organic fashion; it asks us to respect the labour and resources embedded in every item. Buying fewer items is not a punishment; it often means choosing better ones and keeping them longer.", "synthetic shed durable longevity textile"),
    ("Water Conservation at Home", "Promote sensible household water use in dry regions.", "水资源短缺在许多地区日益严重。请分析家庭生活中浪费水的原因，并提出节约办法。", "Water scarcity is becoming a serious concern, yet households often waste enormous quantities without noticing. On the one hand, modern plumbing offers comfort that previous generations could not imagine, and clean running water protects health. Moreover, some regions genuinely have abundant supplies, so excessive alarm is unnecessary everywhere. The point is to match expectations to local reality rather than imposing guilt everywhere. However, long showers, leaking taps and inefficient appliances consume resources that must be treated, pumped and heated. Consequently, both bills and environmental pressure rise, especially during droughts. On the other hand, individual thrift alone cannot fix badly managed infrastructure, since leaking pipes may lose more water than all household care. Effective action therefore requires utilities to repair networks and governments to price water fairly while protecting basic needs for the poor. Families can install efficient fixtures, reuse greywater for plants and fix drips promptly. Schools can teach children how much water a short shower saves. In conclusion, conserving water is a shared duty combining personal habits with responsible management.", "Running water brings comfort and hygiene, but long showers, leaks and inefficient appliances waste treated resources and strain supplies. Household thrift is not enough when pipes leak, so utilities must repair networks and governments price water fairly. Clean running water protects health and should never be denied to the poor, but long showers and leaking appliances waste it. Household effort cannot compensate for broken pipes, so utilities must repair networks and pricing must be fair. Efficient fixtures, greywater reuse and practical education complete the solution.", "Turning on a tap feels effortless, which is precisely why few people think about where the water came from. Yet in many regions rivers are falling and underground reserves are shrinking. Clearly, everyone needs clean water for drinking and sanitation, and no solution should deny that basic right. Waste, however, is different from need: sprinklers on wet lawns and half-empty washing machines squander a resource that others lack. I would prioritise two responses. First, water companies must replace ageing pipes and publish leakage figures. Second, households can adopt modest habits such as shorter showers, full loads and collecting rainwater for gardens. Schools should help children understand the water cycle in practical terms. When communities face shortages together, fair rules are easier to accept. A resource shared fairly is also a resource people willingly protect. Ultimately, conservation works best when personal effort is matched by reliable public systems.", "scarcity inefficient droughts thrift fixtures"),
    ("Towards a Circular Economy", "Explain reuse and recycling systems beyond disposal.", "传统的生产消费模式是取、造、弃。请说明循环经济的好处，并谈谈如何实现。", "Most products still follow a linear path from extraction to landfill, but a circular economy offers a smarter alternative. On the one hand, recycling and reuse reduce the demand for raw materials and cut pollution from manufacturing. Moreover, repairing goods creates local skilled jobs and keeps money within communities. A second-hand market also allows low-income families to obtain quality items. However, circular systems require careful design, since many products are glued shut or made from mixed materials that cannot be separated. Consequently, good intentions often end in incineration. On the other hand, not every item can realistically be recycled, and overly complex rules may confuse households. A workable approach starts with producers, who should design for durability and easy disassembly. Governments can set collection targets and make manufacturers responsible for disposal costs. Consumers, in turn, need clear labelling and convenient drop-off points. Reuse should always come before recycling, since reprocessing materials still consumes energy. Prevention is cheaper than collection. In conclusion, waste should be redesigned out of the system rather than managed after it appears.", "A circular economy reuses materials, cuts raw extraction and supports repair jobs, yet glued products and mixed materials defeat recycling. Producers should design for durability and disassembly; governments need targets and manufacturer responsibility; consumers need clear labels. Waste is best prevented by design. Reusing materials reduces extraction and pollution, and repair shops create local work. Unfortunately, glued components and mixed plastics make many products impossible to recover, so good intentions end in incineration. Producers must design for disassembly, governments should set targets, and shoppers need simple labels and collection points.", "The phrase take, make, waste describes our economy with uncomfortable accuracy. Resources are extracted, turned into products and discarded within a few years. A circular model instead keeps materials in use for as long as possible. The advantages are clear: lower emissions, less landfill and more resilient supply chains. Nevertheless, enthusiasm should not hide practical difficulties. Recycling mixed plastics remains expensive, and consumers often misunderstand which items belong in which bin. I would begin with the design stage, because decisions made in a factory determine whether repair is possible later. Extended producer responsibility would encourage companies to think carefully. Local repair cafes and shared tool libraries can change habits at community level. The aim is not perfect sorting at the kitchen bin but fewer products designed to become rubbish. Ultimately, the goal is not perfect recycling but fewer things that must be thrown away.", "extraction landfill disassembly durability incineration"),
    ("Food Waste and Sustainable Diets", "Connect daily food choices with environmental impact.", "大量食物被丢弃，同时畜牧业也带来环境压力。请分析饮食与可持续生活的关系。", "Food choices affect the planet far more than most people realise, from farming methods to rubbish. On the one hand, plentiful affordable food is a genuine achievement that improves health and reduces hardship. Moreover, diverse diets bring pleasure and reflect cultural identity. Food is also a source of comfort and tradition that should not be dismissed lightly. However, roughly a third of food is wasted, while livestock farming consumes land and water and produces significant emissions. Consequently, discarded meals waste every resource used to grow and transport them. On the other hand, asking everyone to adopt the same diet ignores income, tradition and nutritional needs. Realistic change means reducing waste first: planning meals, storing food properly and using leftovers creatively. Shifting moderately towards plant-based proteins would also lower pressure without demanding perfection. Governments can help by clarifying date labels and improving composting. Restaurants could offer smaller portions and allow customers to take leftovers home. In conclusion, sustainable eating is less about dramatic restriction than about respect for the resources behind every meal.", "Affordable, varied food improves wellbeing, but widespread waste and livestock emissions strain land, water and climate. Rather than imposing identical diets, we should plan meals, store food well and use leftovers, while moderately increasing plant-based proteins. Clearer labels and composting help. Sustainable eating means respecting resources. Cheap varied food improves health and carries cultural meaning, so lecturing people about their plates is unhelpful. Waste and livestock emissions nevertheless put pressure on land and climate. Planning meals, storing food properly and eating more plant-based proteins reduce that pressure gradually, while clearer labels and composting support the change.", "A meal thrown away represents wasted land, labour, water and fuel, yet many households do it weekly. Abundant food is valuable and should not be taken for granted, especially after centuries of scarcity. The environmental cost, however, is substantial: agriculture occupies huge areas and livestock generates methane. I believe the simplest improvements concern behaviour rather than politics. Writing a shopping list, understanding use-by dates and freezing surplus can cut waste sharply. Eating more beans and vegetables a few times a week reduces pressure without eliminating favourite foods. Restaurants, too, should offer smaller portions. At a wider level, governments could discourage landfill disposal of organic matter. None of these steps requires heroism. Good food deserves respect, and respect begins with not throwing it away. That attitude is easier to teach than any strict rule. In the end, a sustainable diet is one that feeds people well while treating the resources behind food with care.", "livestock emissions leftovers composting nutritional"),
])


# UNIT 5
UNITS.append([
    ("Why Traditions Matter", "Explores the value of traditions in modern life", "为什么传统习俗对现代社会仍然重要？请说明传统如何帮助人们建立归属感，以及年轻人为何可能对其失去兴趣，并谈谈应该如何对待传统。", "Traditions give people a sense of belonging that is difficult to replace in modern life. They connect individuals to their families, communities and history, creating a stable identity in a rapidly changing world. However, many young people today show less interest in inherited customs, largely because these practices can seem outdated or disconnected from their daily reality. Moreover, migration and global media often weaken local habits, as newer influences appear more attractive and convenient. Consequently, some traditions gradually disappear, taking with them valuable knowledge and shared memories. On the other hand, traditions should never be preserved rigidly or imposed on those who reject them. A healthier approach is to let communities adapt their customs voluntarily, keeping the meaning while changing the form. For instance, preparing a festival meal together teaches patience and cooperation, while retelling family stories keeps grandparents present in the lives of children. In my view, traditions matter not because they are old, but because they remind us who we are. Even a simple annual meal can strengthen relationships that busy modern schedules otherwise allow to fade.", "Traditions provide belonging and link people to family, community and history, giving stability amid change. Yet many young people lose interest because customs feel outdated and irrelevant to modern life. Migration and global media further weaken local habits by offering attractive alternatives, so practices vanish along with shared memories. Still, traditions should not be preserved rigidly or forced on others. Communities ought to adapt them freely, maintaining meaning while changing outward form. Their real value lies in reminding us of our shared identity and origins, not merely in their age. Such customs also teach cooperation and patience across generations.", "Young generations frequently distance themselves from traditional practices because modern life moves quickly and old rituals may feel meaningless. Nevertheless, such customs offer emotional security and preserve collective memory that would otherwise be lost. Global culture and migration can dilute local identity, yet they can also introduce fresh forms that keep traditions alive. Therefore, preservation should be flexible rather than strict: people need freedom to reinterpret ceremonies and symbols. Schools can also invite pupils to investigate local ceremonies, turning passive observation into genuine understanding. In conclusion, traditions remain essential when they help individuals understand their roots while allowing room for personal choice and contemporary creativity. When a community decides together which elements still carry meaning, the result is a living practice rather than a performance staged for outsiders. Older and younger members can then share responsibility for keeping the celebration alive, rather than treating it as somebody else's duty.", "belonging inherited customs outdated disappear"),
    ("Language and Identity", "Discusses how language shapes personal and cultural identity", "语言如何影响人的文化认同？请说明母语为何重要，双语者可能面临怎样的身份困惑，以及社会应如何看待方言或少数民族语言。", "Language is far more than a practical tool; it shapes how people think and understand themselves. A mother tongue carries childhood memories, emotional expressions and the values of a community, so losing it can feel like losing part of one's identity. Moreover, bilingual individuals often experience confusion, because they may feel they belong fully to neither cultural group. However, this inner conflict can also become a strength, allowing them to move between worlds and understand different perspectives. Consequently, societies that respect linguistic diversity tend to be more inclusive and creative. On the other hand, dominant languages are often treated as the only path to success, which pressures minority speakers to abandon their heritage. For example, a grandparent who tells stories in a regional dialect passes on humour and wisdom that translation cannot fully capture. When a language is neglected, younger speakers lose access to jokes, songs and family histories that carry emotional weight. In my opinion, schools and governments should protect dialects and minority languages while still teaching widely used languages. Schools that teach regional expressions alongside a standard language show pupils that speaking differently is not a sign of inferiority.", "Language shapes identity because a mother tongue holds childhood memories, emotions and community values; losing it means losing part of oneself. Bilingual people may feel torn between cultures, belonging fully to neither, yet this conflict can become an advantage by building empathy and flexibility. Meanwhile, societies valuing diversity become more inclusive, whereas pressure to adopt only a dominant language threatens heritage. Therefore schools and governments should safeguard dialects and minority languages alongside widely spoken ones, since variety strengthens culture. Public recognition of local speech strengthens confidence.", "People express who they are mainly through the language they grew up with, which carries deep emotions and family history. When someone speaks two languages, they may struggle to decide which culture truly defines them, but the same situation also gives them a wider outlook. Unfortunately, economic pressure often pushes minority speakers to abandon their native tongue in favour of a global one. As a result, unique expressions and traditional knowledge disappear. Public signs, local radio and community classes can make minority languages visible in everyday life rather than only in museums. I believe the best solution is to celebrate multilingualism publicly and support minority languages in education, proving that different languages can coexist and strengthen one another. When children hear their home language in books and broadcasts, they are less likely to associate it with failure or shame. A society that values several languages gives its citizens a richer cultural vocabulary and a stronger sense of mutual belonging.", "mother tongue bilingual diversity heritage"),
    ("Festivals Across Generations", "Examines changing celebrations and their social role", "节日庆祝方式为何会在代际之间发生变化？请分析商业化的影响、家庭团聚的意义，以及如何让传统节日吸引年轻人。", "Festivals change from one generation to the next because the societies around them change. Older people often remember celebrations as quiet family gatherings rooted in custom, while younger people tend to expect entertainment, travel and shared experiences online. Commercialisation is a major cause of this shift, since businesses turn meaningful occasions into opportunities for shopping and advertising. However, commerce is not purely negative; it can also make traditions visible and accessible to wider audiences. Moreover, the core value of most festivals remains family reunion, which offers emotional support and a sense of continuity. Consequently, when celebrations become only about spending, their deeper meaning quickly weakens. On the other hand, younger generations are not indifferent to heritage; they simply prefer active participation over passive ritual. Families can also record older relatives describing how a celebration once looked, giving young people a personal connection that advertising cannot imitate. In my view, festivals will stay alive if families explain their stories and allow young people to reshape how they are celebrated. Young people also care about environmental and ethical issues, so festivals that address these concerns may feel more relevant.", "Festivals evolve because society changes: elders recall quiet family customs, while the young expect entertainment and online sharing. Commercialisation turns occasions into shopping events, yet it can also spread awareness. The lasting value of festivals lies in family reunion and shared continuity across generations, but excessive spending erodes meaning. Young people are not uninterested; they favour active involvement over passive ritual. Thus festivals survive when families pass on stories and permit younger generations to modernise celebrations. Creative workshops can give the young an active role.", "Modern festivals differ sharply from earlier ones because commerce and technology reshape expectations. Advertisers encourage gift buying, which sometimes overshadows the original purpose of a celebration. Nevertheless, commercial attention can revive forgotten customs and attract tourists. What matters most is the reunion of relatives, since it strengthens bonds across generations. Music, cooking and storytelling workshops let children experience a festival actively instead of merely watching adults perform familiar duties. To engage the young, communities should create interactive events, invite creative input and explain the history behind rituals. Overall, traditions remain meaningful only when people understand them and adapt their form to contemporary life. Documenting recipes and songs before older relatives pass away gives families a precious record that commerce cannot manufacture. Community organisers should invite teenagers to design part of the programme, because ownership is more powerful than instruction from adults who may no longer understand their interests.", "commercialisation reunion continuity participation celebrations"),
    ("Immigrant Cultural Identity", "Considers the identity challenges faced by immigrants", "移民如何在保留本土文化和融入新社会之间取得平衡？请讨论代际差异、语言障碍以及多元文化环境的利弊。", "Immigrants often struggle to balance their original culture with the demands of a new society. The first generation usually keeps strong ties to its homeland, maintaining familiar food, language and celebrations, yet it may face serious language barriers at work and in public life. Their children, on the other hand, grow up between two cultures and may feel torn between parents expectations and the norms of their peers. Moreover, multicultural neighbourhoods can provide comfort and practical support, helping newcomers feel less isolated. However, they may also encourage separation if communities never interact with one another. Consequently, successful integration requires both effort from immigrants and openness from the host society. A newcomer who speaks the local language at work yet uses the family language at home often develops two complementary ways of seeing the world. Without access to both, people may feel they must betray one community in order to succeed in another. In my opinion, people should be free to keep their heritage while participating fully in civic life. Workplaces and schools should recognise qualifications earned abroad, since wasted skills damage both the individual and the wider community.", "Immigrants balance old and new cultures with difficulty. The first generation preserves homeland habits but faces serious language barriers, while their children live between family expectations and local norms. Multicultural areas offer emotional support yet may cause separation when groups never mix. Therefore integration demands sustained effort from newcomers and genuine openness from the host community. People can maintain their heritage and join civic life simultaneously, since identity is flexible rather than a simple choice between two sides. Equal access to language classes speeds this process.", "People who move abroad rarely abandon their former identity completely. Older immigrants often rely on familiar traditions for comfort, although limited language skills can restrict their opportunities. Younger family members adapt faster and sometimes feel embarrassed by their parents customs, which creates tension at home. Living among fellow countrymen provides emotional safety, but excessive separation prevents real integration. Community centres and mixed schools give children from different backgrounds a chance to cooperate, and friendships formed there often challenge stereotypes more effectively than speeches. At the same time, parents need reasons to feel that their own culture is respected rather than merely tolerated. I believe governments should promote interaction through schools and public events, allowing immigrants to keep their roots while building a shared local identity. Policies that protect both rights and shared responsibilities help newcomers feel that belonging does not require self-erasure.", "homeland barriers norms integration civic"),
    ("Art as Cultural Expression", "Explores how art expresses shared cultural values", "艺术如何体现一个民族的文化价值？请说明传统艺术形式的意义、全球化带来的影响，以及是否应投入公共资金支持艺术。", "Art acts as a powerful record of a culture's values, emotions and history. Traditional forms such as folk painting, dance or theatre carry stories that words alone cannot express, and they help communities recognise shared experiences. Moreover, these art forms often develop from everyday life, so they reveal how ordinary people understood beauty and suffering. However, globalisation has changed this picture considerably, as international trends can overshadow local styles and push artists towards familiar international patterns. Consequently, distinctive cultural expressions may disappear if they cannot compete commercially. On the other hand, global exchange also introduces new techniques and audiences, giving traditional artists unexpected opportunities. Traditional crafts and performances also require patience and long training, qualities that are easily undervalued in a culture of instant results. Museums and festivals can therefore act as bridges, showing why an old technique mattered and how it still speaks to contemporary concerns. In my view, public funding for the arts is justified because cultural heritage cannot survive on market forces alone. Creative artists who reinterpret old motifs often attract younger audiences and keep a visual language alive.", "Art preserves a culture's values and history through stories that ordinary language cannot convey, revealing community experiences, struggles and imagination. Traditional forms arise from everyday life, but globalisation can overshadow local styles and make unique expressions disappear commercially. Still, international exchange brings fresh methods and wider audiences to artists. Public money is therefore necessary to protect heritage, although it should encourage living creativity instead of freezing art in earlier forms. This requires curiosity and respect for the communities concerned.", "Artistic work reflects what a society considers beautiful, painful or sacred. Folk traditions pass on collective memories and teach younger generations about their ancestors. Today global popular culture dominates many markets, making local artists struggle for attention. This trend is worrying, but cross-cultural contact can also inspire original combinations. Young artists who combine inherited techniques with modern subjects often reach audiences that older institutions cannot. Governments can help by funding education and studio spaces, yet they should avoid dictating what authentic culture must look like. Ultimately, a tradition stays alive when its makers are free to question and transform it. Funding bodies should judge proposals by their quality and honesty, not by how obediently they repeat familiar images of the past. Support should also reach independent artists, since they often experiment with forms that large institutions are afraid to fund without guaranteed success.", "heritage globalisation overshadow distinctive audiences"),
    ("Literature and Cultural Memory", "Discusses the role of literature in preserving memory", "文学在保存民族文化记忆方面起什么作用？请分析经典作品的价值、翻译的意义，以及年轻人不读经典的原因。", "Literature keeps a culture's memory alive by recording how people thought, argued and imagined at particular moments in history. Classic novels and poems preserve language, social customs and moral debates, offering later generations a direct connection to earlier minds. Moreover, reading such works develops empathy, because literature invites us to see the world through unfamiliar eyes. However, many young people avoid classics today, finding their language difficult and their concerns distant from modern life. Consequently, works that once shaped national identity risk being read only by specialists. On the other hand, translation can bring national literature to international readers, though it inevitably changes rhythm and nuance. When students read a novel from another period, they notice both unfamiliar assumptions and emotions that remain surprisingly familiar. This double vision is exactly what helps them understand their own society more clearly. In my view, classics should be taught as living conversations rather than sacred monuments. Reading widely also improves vocabulary and gives students models for expressing complicated feelings.", "Literature preserves cultural memory by capturing past thought, customs and moral debates, while developing empathy through unfamiliar viewpoints. Classic novels and poems may feel remote, yet they connect readers to earlier generations. Translation brings these works to new audiences but inevitably changes rhythm and nuance. Therefore classics should be presented as living discussions, not fixed monuments, so students can question them and relate them to current issues. Personal responses matter more than memorised summaries. Different readers may notice different meanings.", "Great books function as archives of collective experience, showing how earlier societies faced familiar human problems. Their language may feel strange, but the emotions and conflicts remain recognisable. Unfortunately, school methods often turn reading into examination practice, which destroys pleasure and curiosity. Translations help foreign readers access national treasures, although some beauty is always lost. A poem remembered from childhood can carry a whole period's hopes and fears. Schools sometimes spoil this by demanding technical analysis before pupils have enjoyed the text at all. Translation deserves equal care, since a clumsy version can make a masterpiece appear dull. I believe teachers should link classic texts to modern concerns and allow personal interpretation. Teachers might let pupils choose which passages to discuss, so that reading becomes a genuine encounter rather than an imposed duty. Reading groups and public libraries can make older texts feel social again, turning a private difficulty into a shared conversation.", "classics empathy translation nuance monuments"),
    ("Identity in a Global City", "Examines how city life shapes cultural identity", "全球化城市生活如何影响个人的文化身份？请讨论多元邻里、消费文化以及归属感的变化。", "Life in a global city constantly reshapes personal identity because people encounter unfamiliar traditions every day. Multicultural neighbourhoods expose residents to different foods, languages and beliefs, which can broaden their outlook and reduce prejudice. Moreover, cities often celebrate difference publicly through festivals and markets, making hybrid identities feel normal. However, consumer culture can turn cultural difference into a product, encouraging people to perform a simplified version of their heritage for others. Consequently, identity risks becoming a matter of appearance rather than genuine understanding. On the other hand, anonymity in large cities allows individuals to experiment and choose which influences they want to adopt. A person may celebrate a national holiday with colleagues, observe a religious custom at home and follow global trends online without finding these roles contradictory. The challenge is to move beyond surfaces, because tasting a food is not the same as understanding the history behind it. In my view, urban life offers both freedom and superficiality, and a strong sense of belonging emerges only when contact develops into real relationships. People then learn to appreciate difference without pretending that every tradition is interchangeable.", "Global cities reshape identity through daily contact with diverse traditions, widening perspectives and challenging prejudice. Public festivals make hybrid identities acceptable, yet consumer culture may reduce heritage to a marketable image, replacing real understanding with appearance. At the same time, urban anonymity gives people freedom to select influences. Genuine belonging grows when brief encounters develop into lasting relationships instead of remaining superficial. Local history projects and shared neighbourhood events can deepen such understanding considerably. Over time, such contact changes attitudes.", "City dwellers meet many cultures at once, and this exposure can make them more tolerant and adaptable. Restaurants, street festivals and mixed schools create everyday opportunities for learning. Nevertheless, cities also sell culture as entertainment, so complex traditions may become stereotypes. Another difficulty is that constant movement weakens long-term friendships, leaving people emotionally isolated despite crowds. Newcomers often change their accents, clothes and habits to fit in, then wonder which version feels authentic. Cities that provide community spaces and open conversations help them combine influences honestly. Without such spaces, difference remains a spectacle rather than a shared life. I think meaningful identity in a city depends on building a few deep connections. Cities that invest in libraries, parks and community kitchens give residents neutral places where friendship can cross cultural boundaries. Small everyday interactions may appear unimportant, but repeated contact gradually turns strangers into neighbours and neighbours into friends.", "multicultural hybrid consumer identity belonging"),
    ("Family Customs and Change", "Looks at evolving family traditions", "家庭习俗为什么会随着时间改变？请说明代际沟通、技术进步和家庭仪式感的作用，并判断是否应该保留旧习惯。", "Family customs change over time because each generation adapts inherited habits to its own circumstances. Parents often treasure familiar rituals, such as shared meals or holiday preparations, since these create continuity and emotional security. However, children may view the same routines as restrictive or irrelevant, particularly when technology reshapes how families communicate. Moreover, busy schedules and geographic distance make older patterns difficult to maintain. Consequently, some rituals quietly disappear, and with them goes a valuable opportunity for conversation. On the other hand, preserving every old habit is unrealistic and may create resentment rather than closeness. A shared photograph album or a monthly video call may be modern, but it can still express the same care as an old family dinner. What matters is that everyone has a voice in deciding which habits remain meaningful. Rituals imposed from above rarely survive for long, whereas those chosen together often grow stronger. In my opinion, families should keep the spirit of their customs while changing their form. Listening to relatives explain why a custom mattered can transform an obligation into a cherished habit.", "Family customs evolve as each generation adjusts old habits to new conditions. Parents value shared rituals for continuity, whereas children may find them restrictive, especially when technology changes communication and busy lives separate relatives. Some routines vanish, taking chances for conversation with them. Yet keeping every habit is unrealistic and can breed resentment. Families should preserve the affection behind rituals while modernising their form, since genuine attention matters more than exact repetition. Shared decisions keep family life warm and flexible.", "Household traditions rarely remain fixed because life circumstances shift constantly. Older relatives often see rituals as proof of care, while younger members may prefer informal contact through messages and video calls. Distance and workload make regular gatherings harder, so meaningful customs can fade. Still, forcing outdated routines is unlikely to produce warmth. Older relatives may feel hurt when a familiar routine is dropped, so explanations matter as much as the decision itself. Children, in turn, should learn the stories behind practices they find tedious. Negotiation turns a potential conflict into a chance for mutual understanding. I believe the healthiest solution is to decide together which practices still express love and replace the rest with new, simpler habits. A tradition that adapts to new circumstances is not weakened; it proves that the family still cares enough to keep practising it. The aim is not to please everyone equally, but to keep a genuine conversation alive across generations.", "continuity restrictive rituals customs obligation"),
    ("National Holidays and Memory", "Analyzes the historical function of national holidays", "国家节日如何塑造集体记忆？请说明纪念活动的教育意义、政治利用的风险，以及个人应如何在节日中思考历史。", "National holidays shape collective memory by setting aside time for a society to remember shared historical experiences. Ceremonies, speeches and public symbols teach citizens about sacrifices and achievements that might otherwise be forgotten. Moreover, such occasions can strengthen social unity and encourage gratitude towards earlier generations. However, political leaders may use these commemorations selectively, emphasising comfortable stories while ignoring painful or inconvenient facts. Consequently, holidays can simplify history into a flattering national myth rather than an honest reflection. On the other hand, critical citizens can use the same occasion to ask difficult questions and include voices that were previously silenced. Citizens can read speeches critically, compare competing accounts and discuss uncomfortable chapters with their children. A mature commemoration admits that pride and shame may coexist in the same national story. Only then does a public holiday become an occasion for thought rather than simple celebration. In my view, remembrance is valuable only when it accepts complexity. Honest education about the past helps young people inherit responsibility without inheriting hatred.", "National holidays create collective memory through ceremonies that teach citizens about shared sacrifices and achievements, promoting unity and gratitude. Yet politicians may select flattering stories and hide uncomfortable truths, turning remembrance into myth. Critical citizens can use these days to ask hard questions and include ignored voices. Commemoration is worthwhile only when it accepts complexity, combining honest pride with responsibility. Schools and museums play a key role by presenting several viewpoints and encouraging pupils to question simple stories.", "Public holidays are powerful educational tools because they bring history into ordinary conversation. Families and schools retell stories that define a nation and honour those who suffered. Nevertheless, official versions often highlight victory and omit defeat or injustice, which distorts understanding. This is dangerous because simplified pride can prevent honest debate. Schools can invite pupils to examine photographs, letters and oral testimonies from several viewpoints. This turns a ceremonial day into genuine historical inquiry. Although such reflection may be uncomfortable, it builds a more honest community than silent agreement. I believe individuals should treat holidays as opportunities for reflection, reading beyond official speeches and acknowledging both admirable and shameful chapters. When a nation faces its mistakes openly, remembrance can build trust rather than resentment among citizens. Citizens who understand the past are better equipped to resist propaganda and to defend democratic values in the present.", "collective memory flattering myth complexity"),
    ("Cultural Exchange and Respect", "Considers the ethics of intercultural contact", "文化交流如何才能真正建立在相互尊重之上？请分析刻板印象、文化挪用和双向学习的重要性。", "Genuine cultural exchange depends on respect rather than simple curiosity. When people encounter a foreign tradition, they often rely on stereotypes that reduce a rich culture to a few recognisable images. Moreover, modern media accelerate this problem by spreading simplified symbols far faster than accurate knowledge. Consequently, borrowing unfamiliar clothing, music or religious signs can become cultural appropriation, in which a dominant group profits from meanings it does not understand. On the other hand, exchange can be enriching when it is based on equal dialogue and honest acknowledgement of origins. A visitor who learns a few words of the local language and asks about the meaning of a ceremony shows more respect than one who simply takes photographs. Such small gestures turn observation into a relationship. In my opinion, people should ask who benefits from a cultural practice and whether its original community has been consulted. True respect means listening as much as borrowing, and accepting that some traditions are not ours to use. Museums and schools can present a culture from within, explaining its history instead of displaying it as exotic decoration.", "Cultural exchange requires respect, yet stereotypes reduce complex cultures to a few images, especially through fast-spreading media. Borrowing symbols without understanding can become appropriation when powerful groups profit from unfamiliar meanings. However, equal dialogue and acknowledgement of origins make exchange enriching. People should consider who benefits and whether source communities were consulted; real respect involves listening and accepting limits. Education is crucial because it reveals power differences and challenges comfortable assumptions. Curiosity without humility easily becomes exploitation.", "Contact between cultures can broaden horizons, but it often begins with assumptions. Foreign customs are judged through familiar values, so their deeper meaning is missed. When attractive elements are copied for profit without credit, the original community feels exploited and misunderstood. This is avoidable if exchange becomes a two-way process in which both sides explain, question and learn. Tourism can support local artisans when profits return to the community and visitors learn from residents rather than about them. Problems begin when traditions are removed from their setting and sold as decoration. I believe education is essential: students need to recognise power differences and treat cultural practices as living traditions belonging to real people. Artists and writers should be free to borrow forms, but they must credit their sources and share benefits fairly. Respect also means accepting that some ceremonies should remain closed to outsiders, however curious those outsiders may be.", "stereotypes appropriation acknowledgement dialogue respect"),
])
# UNIT 6
UNITS.append([
    ("The Purpose of Schooling", "Questions what education should aim to achieve", "学校教育的主要目标应该是什么？请比较传授知识、培养品格与准备就业之间的重要性。", "The purpose of schooling extends far beyond the simple transfer of information. While knowledge of subjects remains essential, schools should also develop character, curiosity and the ability to think independently. Moreover, employers increasingly value communication and adaptability, which suggests that narrow examination preparation is no longer sufficient. However, some argue that schools must focus primarily on practical skills because young people need secure employment. Consequently, a balanced curriculum is needed, combining academic understanding with personal development and realistic career guidance. A pupil who understands scientific reasoning can evaluate news claims, while one who practises teamwork can work effectively with people holding different views. These abilities are not easily measured by a single examination, yet they shape adult life profoundly. On the other hand, character education can become superficial if it is reduced to posters and slogans rather than daily practice. In my view, the true aim of education is to produce thoughtful, capable individuals who can contribute to society. A school that neglects imagination may produce efficient workers who cannot question the world around them.", "Schooling should do more than transmit facts: it must develop character, curiosity and independent thinking. Employers now demand communication and adaptability, so narrow exam preparation is inadequate. Although practical skills matter for employment, a balanced curriculum combines knowledge, personal growth and career guidance. Character education must be practised daily rather than displayed as slogans. Ultimately, education should form thoughtful, capable people able to contribute to society and live well. Real learning also requires room for mistakes.", "Many people believe school exists mainly to prepare students for work, but this view is too limited. Subjects such as literature or history cultivate imagination and judgement, which benefit society in less visible ways. Nevertheless, young people deserve honest guidance about future careers and financial reality. The danger lies in measuring every lesson by immediate economic value. Subjects such as art and history may not lead directly to a job, but they teach interpretation and sensitivity. Employers repeatedly report that graduates need judgement and communication more than narrow technical facts. I argue that a good school balances usefulness with wider human development, teaching pupils both how to earn a living and how to live well. Examinations remain useful, yet they should measure understanding rather than rewarding the ability to repeat textbook language. A curriculum built only around examinations may quietly narrow what young people believe is worth knowing.", "transfer adaptability curriculum thoughtful curiosity"),
    ("Lifelong Learning", "Explores learning beyond formal education", "为什么终身学习对成年人很重要？请说明职场变化、个人成长以及可能遇到的困难。", "Lifelong learning has become essential because the world of work changes faster than any single qualification can cover. Skills that were valuable a decade ago may now be outdated, so adults must continually update their knowledge to remain employable. Moreover, learning is not only economic; it also supports personal growth, confidence and mental flexibility as people age. A retired engineer who learns photography or a nurse studying a new language proves that education is not restricted to school corridors. Communities and libraries can offer short, affordable courses that fit around adult responsibilities. However, many adults struggle to find time and energy for study while managing jobs and families. Consequently, flexible courses and supportive employers are necessary if continuing education is to be realistic. On the other hand, constant pressure to improve oneself can create anxiety and the feeling that one is never good enough. In my opinion, lifelong learning should be seen as an opportunity rather than an obligation, driven by genuine curiosity. Without such habits, a qualification earned at twenty may lose its value long before retirement.", "Lifelong learning matters because workplaces change rapidly and qualifications become outdated, so adults need constant updating to stay employable. Beyond economics, study supports confidence, curiosity and mental flexibility. Yet jobs and family duties leave little time, making flexible courses and employer support necessary. At the same time, pressure to improve endlessly may cause anxiety. Learning should therefore be an opportunity motivated by genuine interest and real need. Personal curiosity is often the engine of continuous improvement and adaptation.", "Adult education is no longer a luxury but a practical necessity in changing economies. Technology alters entire industries, and workers who stop learning risk exclusion. Still, formal courses are not the only path: reading, workshops and informal exchange all count. The biggest obstacles are fatigue, cost and fear of failure, especially for those who disliked school. Online materials have lowered costs, although motivation remains a problem for learners studying alone. Study groups and workplace training can supply the structure that self-directed courses lack. The reward is not only a better salary but also the confidence to face change. I believe societies should normalise learning at every age and respect different rhythms. Employers can support this by paying for training and recognising learning that happens outside formal institutions. Learning also keeps the mind active and gives people a reason to meet others outside their familiar circle.", "qualification outdated flexible anxiety curiosity"),
    ("Exam Systems: Pros and Cons", "Evaluates the strengths and weaknesses of examinations", "考试制度有哪些优缺点？请分析公平性、应试教育的问题，以及如何改进评估方式。", "Examinations remain the most common method of assessment because they appear fair and efficient. Every candidate faces the same questions under similar conditions, which reduces the influence of personal connections and subjective judgement. Moreover, exams provide clear evidence of academic achievement and help universities select students. However, this system has serious drawbacks, since teaching often narrows to exam techniques and memorisation rather than deep understanding. Consequently, creative thinking and practical ability may be neglected. Feedback from coursework can reveal strengths that a timed paper hides, such as careful research or original argument. Universities also benefit from seeing how a student thinks over time rather than how quickly they answer under pressure. For fairness, every method needs clear criteria and trained examiners. On the other hand, alternative forms of assessment, such as projects or portfolios, can be expensive and vulnerable to bias. In my view, the best solution is a combination of examinations and continuous assessment. A system that rewards deeper thinking will gradually change what happens in classrooms every day.", "Exams seem fair because all candidates face identical questions, limiting favouritism and giving clear evidence of achievement for university selection. Yet they encourage teaching to the test, memorisation and neglect of creativity. Alternatives such as portfolios may be costly and subjective. Therefore a mixed system works best, combining exams for core knowledge with continuous assessment of analysis, collaboration and original work. Clear criteria keep such assessment credible. Clear marking criteria and transparency also help students trust the results.", "Standardised tests create an impression of equality and are relatively simple to organise. They also give students a clear goal and measurable feedback. Unfortunately, when results determine nearly everything, classrooms focus on predicting answers instead of asking questions. This harms curiosity and deep learning. Coursework offers a broader picture but can favour students with supportive families. Oral presentations test communication that written papers cannot capture. Group projects reveal cooperation, although they must be marked individually to remain fair. No single format can measure intelligence completely, so variety is the safest principle. I suggest using several methods together, so no single score defines a person's ability. Until that happens, teachers should explain why a task was assessed in a particular way and how feedback can be used. Teachers, meanwhile, need protection from excessive paperwork so that they can discuss feedback with pupils properly and adjust their teaching accordingly.", "examinations memorisation portfolios bias continuous"),
    ("Online Learning and Motivation", "Examines independent study in digital environments", "在线学习有哪些优势与不足？请讨论自律、师生互动和技术障碍，并提出改善建议。", "Online learning offers remarkable flexibility, allowing students to study at their own pace and revisit difficult material whenever necessary. It also removes geographical barriers, giving access to courses that were once unavailable. For instance, a simulation of a scientific experiment can let pupils repeat a test safely until they understand the result. Teachers can also use short quizzes during a lesson to identify confusion before it becomes frustration. However, this freedom demands a high level of self-discipline, and many learners struggle without the structure of a classroom. Moreover, direct interaction with teachers and classmates is reduced, which can weaken motivation and make questions harder to resolve. Consequently, some students feel isolated and eventually abandon their courses. On the other hand, technology itself is rarely the only problem; poor design and lack of support often matter more. When these habits are established, technology becomes almost invisible and learning remains the focus. In my view, blended courses work best, combining digital resources with regular discussion. Clear guidance about acceptable device use protects attention without rejecting useful innovation.", "Online study is flexible and removes geographical barriers, yet it requires strong self-discipline and often reduces contact with teachers and peers. Isolation and weak structure cause many learners to drop out. Technology is seldom the sole issue, since poor course design and insufficient support play a large role. Blended learning, mixing digital materials with live discussion and firm deadlines, is the most effective model for most students. Motivation grows when progress is visible and tutors respond quickly to questions.", "Digital courses can fit around work and family responsibilities, which makes education accessible to more adults. Recorded lessons also allow repeated review. Nevertheless, watching videos is passive, and without a tutor students may never notice their mistakes. Internet access and suitable devices are additional obstacles in poorer regions. Recorded explanations help pupils review at their own speed, which is especially useful for those learning an additional language. However, passive watching should be replaced by tasks that require predictions and answers. A tutor who responds quickly can prevent a small misunderstanding from becoming a permanent gap. I believe providers should design short interactive tasks and create study groups, so online learners feel guided rather than abandoned. Schools should also teach students to judge online sources critically, because access alone does not guarantee understanding. Finally, schools should provide devices or offline alternatives where necessary, so that poverty does not decide who can study.", "flexibility self-discipline interaction isolated blended"),
    ("Critical Thinking in Class", "Promotes questioning and analysis in education", "为什么批判性思维应成为课堂的核心目标？请说明其好处、教师面临的困难及培养方法。", "Critical thinking should stand at the centre of education because students need to evaluate information rather than merely absorb it. In an age of constant online content, the ability to question evidence and identify bias is essential for informed citizenship. Moreover, analytical discussion helps learners develop confidence in their own reasoned opinions. For example, when two pupils interpret the same photograph differently, asking them to defend their readings reveals hidden assumptions. Over time, such practice makes opinion based on evidence rather than habit. However, teaching thinking is harder than teaching facts, since it requires open debate and tolerance for uncertainty. Many teachers also face pressure to cover a fixed syllabus and prepare pupils for examinations. Consequently, questioning is sometimes treated as a distraction. On the other hand, critical thinking can be developed through ordinary classroom practice, using controversial texts and asking students to justify claims. In my view, schools that encourage respectful disagreement prepare pupils far better for adult life. Students who learn to challenge politely become better colleagues and more responsible citizens.", "Critical thinking enables students to assess evidence and detect bias instead of passively absorbing information, which is vital for citizenship. It also builds confidence in reasoned opinion. Yet teachers face syllabus pressure and find open debate difficult to manage, so questioning is often neglected. Simple methods, such as discussing controversial texts and justifying claims, can solve this. Schools rewarding respectful disagreement prepare pupils well for adulthood. In such classes, evidence should matter more than confidence or social status.", "Young people encounter enormous amounts of information daily, so education must teach them how to judge its reliability. Memorising answers offers no protection against manipulation. Encouraging questions, however, can unsettle teachers accustomed to authority and consumes lesson time. Examinations also reward correct answers rather than thoughtful doubt. Students should learn to distinguish a strong argument from a familiar one, and to change their minds when evidence demands it. This is uncomfortable, but it is the foundation of honest inquiry. Assessment must reward reasoning even when the final conclusion is unconventional. I argue that discussion, debate and evidence-based writing should be built into every subject. Assessment rubrics can reward the quality of questions, not only the correctness of the final answer. Ultimately, an education that avoids difficult questions leaves students unprepared for disagreement in public life and vulnerable to confident but empty rhetoric.", "evaluate bias syllabus questioning discussion"),
    ("Teacher-Student Relationships", "Discusses trust and authority in the classroom", "良好的师生关系对学习有什么影响？请分析信任、课堂权威和沟通方式的作用。", "A positive relationship between teachers and students significantly improves learning outcomes. When pupils trust an educator, they are more willing to ask questions, admit confusion and accept constructive criticism. Moreover, a supportive classroom reduces anxiety, allowing students to concentrate on difficult material rather than fearing embarrassment. A teacher who remembers a pupil's interests can connect difficult material to something that genuinely matters to them. Small acts of recognition often motivate more effort than repeated warnings about future failure. Conversely, public humiliation destroys trust that may take years to rebuild. However, closeness must not eliminate professional boundaries, since teachers still need authority to maintain order and fairness. Consequently, the ideal relationship combines warmth with clear expectations. On the other hand, overly strict discipline can destroy open communication and make pupils hide their difficulties. In my view, teachers should listen genuinely and treat every student with equal respect. Consistency matters too, because pupils quickly notice when rules depend on personal preference rather than fairness. Humour and patience can make authority feel human.", "Trust between teachers and students encourages questions, honesty and acceptance of criticism, while reducing anxiety and improving concentration. Yet warmth needs professional boundaries and fair authority, so the best relationship balances kindness with clear expectations. Excessive strictness blocks communication and hides problems. Teachers should listen, justify rules and show equal respect, since dignity motivates better than fear. When pupils feel understood, they are also more likely to persevere through difficulty. Fair recognition of effort also builds trust.", "Students learn more effectively when they feel seen and respected by their teacher. Friendly contact builds confidence and makes failure less threatening. Nevertheless, a teacher cannot simply be a friend, because classrooms require structure and impartial decisions. Problems arise when authority is expressed through intimidation rather than explanation. Fairness is equally important, because favouritism quickly undermines credibility. When discipline is explained rather than imposed, students learn self-control instead of mere obedience. Trust does not mean lowering standards; it means believing that pupils can meet them. I believe respectful dialogue is the key to a productive classroom. When correction is private and specific, students are more likely to accept it and change their behaviour. Both parties then feel able to speak honestly, which is the foundation of any productive working relationship in a school setting. Consistency and kindness are not opposites.", "boundaries authority criticism trust respect"),
    ("Specialisation or Broad Education", "Debates early specialisation versus general study", "学生应该尽早专攻某一领域，还是接受广泛教育？请比较两种方式的利弊并给出观点。", "Students face increasing pressure to specialise early, yet a broad education offers advantages that should not be overlooked. Focusing on one subject from a young age allows deeper expertise and may create a clear path towards a demanding career. Moreover, talent in fields such as music or science often requires years of intensive practice. However, narrow specialisation can limit flexibility, particularly when job markets change unexpectedly. Consequently, students who know only one area may struggle to adapt. Early specialisation also places enormous pressure on teenagers who have not yet discovered what genuinely interests them. A broad curriculum, by contrast, allows unexpected talents to emerge and teaches students how different fields connect. Later choices made from experience tend to be wiser than those made under pressure. On the other hand, general education sometimes lacks depth and fails to prepare anyone for a specific profession. In my opinion, the best approach is broad study first, followed by gradual specialisation. Universities can then offer deeper study without forcing a permanent decision at sixteen.", "Early specialisation builds deep expertise and suits fields needing long practice, such as science or music. However, it reduces flexibility when employment patterns shift, leaving narrowly trained students unable to adapt. Broad education avoids this problem but may lack depth and career preparation. The strongest model offers wide study first and gradual specialisation later, once genuine interests emerge. Guidance should support choice rather than impose it. Good advice and varied experiences help pupils choose realistically rather than following fashion.", "Some pupils know their passion early and benefit from focused training, while others discover it only after exploring several subjects. Specialising too soon can close doors and create regret if the chosen field proves unsuitable. A wide curriculum, in contrast, develops transferable skills and curiosity, though it may feel unfocused. Some gifted musicians and athletes clearly benefit from intensive training from a young age, and their experience should be respected. For most pupils, however, exploring several disciplines builds confidence and reveals unexpected strengths. Universities can then offer deeper study without forcing a permanent decision too early. I favour a staged system, supported by advice rather than pressure. A flexible route also allows people to return to education later, when their interests have become clearer. Breadth should not be confused with randomness; a good general curriculum still has clear goals and high standards.", "expertise flexibility specialisation adapt talent"),
    ("Learning from Failure", "Examines how mistakes contribute to education", "失败在学习中扮演什么角色？请说明害怕犯错的危害、反馈的重要性及如何建立积极的错误观。", "Failure is an unavoidable part of genuine learning, yet many educational environments treat mistakes as shameful. When students fear being wrong, they avoid challenging tasks and choose safe answers, which prevents intellectual growth. Moreover, anxiety about grades can reduce creativity and honest experimentation. When a teacher explains that a wrong answer reveals a specific misunderstanding, students begin to see errors as information rather than judgement. Revision then becomes purposeful, because the learner knows exactly what to change. Classmates can also benefit when mistakes are discussed openly instead of being hidden. This atmosphere requires patience, but it produces students who are willing to attempt difficult problems. On the other hand, mistakes provide valuable information, showing exactly where understanding breaks down. Consequently, timely and constructive feedback matters far more than punishment. In my view, schools should celebrate intelligent failure, provided students reflect on what went wrong. A supportive teacher can turn a small error into a useful discussion that benefits the whole class. This takes honesty and patience.", "Fear of mistakes makes pupils avoid challenge and select safe answers, blocking growth and creativity. Yet errors reveal gaps in understanding, so constructive feedback is more useful than punishment. Teachers who discuss mistakes help students improve step by step. Schools should therefore accept intelligent failure when learners reflect, learn and try again. Grades that recognise effort and progress encourage resilience better than marks alone. Mistakes should be treated as evidence about learning, not as proof of low ability.", "Many students associate errors with low ability rather than with the learning process. This belief discourages risk-taking and hides genuine confusion from teachers. Improvement depends on feedback that explains why an answer failed and how to progress. A classroom culture where drafts and revisions are normal helps pupils develop resilience. Many adults remember a humiliating correction from childhood and still avoid unfamiliar tasks because of it. A different approach is to value first attempts and reward improvement over time. Students who are allowed to revise work learn to take responsibility for learning. I believe grades should sometimes reward effort, not only final correctness. Parents also have a role, since their reaction to a poor grade shapes a child's willingness to try again. Over time, such habits create learners who are less afraid of difficulty and more willing to search for better strategies.", "mistakes feedback punishment reflect attempt"),
    ("Education and Inequality", "Analyzes how background affects educational opportunity", "家庭背景如何影响教育机会？请讨论资源差异、学校作用以及社会可以采取的措施。", "Family background continues to shape educational opportunity to a remarkable degree. Children from wealthy homes often attend better schools, own useful technology and receive private support, while disadvantaged pupils may lack quiet study space or reliable internet. Moreover, parents with higher education can navigate systems confidently and offer academic guidance. A child who shares a bedroom with several relatives may find concentration difficult, while a neighbour with private tutoring gains extra confidence. Such differences accumulate over years and then appear misleadingly as differences in ability. Recognising this pattern is the first step towards fairer policy. Resources must reach the pupils who need them most. Consequently, unequal starting points become lasting achievement gaps. On the other hand, schools can reduce some of this disadvantage through excellent teaching, free resources and early intervention. In my view, societies must combine fair school funding with family support. Early childhood support is especially effective, because gaps widen quickly once formal schooling begins and competition increases. Fairness must be measured by results, not intentions.", "Wealthier families provide better schools, technology and guidance, while poorer children may lack study space or internet, producing achievement gaps. Good teaching and early support in schools can narrow these differences, but cannot undo poverty alone. Fair funding, tutoring, meals and family services are needed so that talent is not wasted. Equal opportunity therefore requires sustained investment and honest measurement of results. Transparent data can reveal where help is needed and whether resources actually reach disadvantaged pupils.", "A child's chances are often decided before entering the classroom. Books at home, stable housing and confident parents all influence performance, yet these advantages are distributed unevenly. Schools can compensate with high expectations and extra help, especially in early years. However, reform must extend beyond education to healthcare and employment. Books in the home, stable housing and confident parents influence performance long before examinations begin. Schools can compensate through high expectations and extra help, especially during the early years. Reform must also address healthcare and employment, because education cannot carry the whole burden. I argue that equal opportunity requires sustained investment in disadvantaged areas. Political will matters as much as money, because unequal systems often benefit groups that resist change. Measures such as free meals and targeted tutoring are practical expressions of a belief that every child can learn given the right conditions.", "background opportunity achievement tutoring disadvantage"),
    ("Studying for Understanding", "Compares deep learning with memorisation", "为什么理解式学习比死记硬背更有效？请解释两者的区别、长期效果及相应的学习方法。", "Learning for understanding is more effective than memorisation because connected knowledge is easier to recall and apply. When students grasp why a formula or argument works, they can transfer it to unfamiliar problems. In contrast, memorised facts learned for an examination are often forgotten within weeks. Moreover, deep understanding encourages genuine interest, whereas repetition quickly becomes boring. For instance, a student who can explain why a historical event happened is more likely to remember its details than one who memorised a date list. Teachers can check understanding by asking pupils to teach a concept back to them. However, some basic information does need to be remembered, such as vocabulary or key dates, so memorisation is not entirely useless. Consequently, the two approaches should work together rather than compete. On the other hand, teachers can promote understanding through explanation, examples and discussion. In my view, education should prioritise meaning, with rehearsal used only as a supporting tool. When pupils explain their reasoning aloud, misconceptions become visible and can be corrected immediately.", "Understanding beats memorisation because connected knowledge is easier to remember and apply to new situations, while exam facts fade quickly and repetition kills interest. Still, vocabulary and dates require some recall, so both methods can coexist. Teachers encourage depth through examples and explanation, asking students to reformulate ideas. Meaning should lead, with rehearsal as a supporting tool. This balance makes learning both durable and efficient. Repeated practice in varied situations then strengthens genuine understanding and memory.", "Students who merely repeat information may pass a test yet understand little. True learning involves explaining relationships, questioning assumptions and using knowledge in different contexts. This takes more time and feels harder, but it lasts longer. Rote learning remains useful for automatic skills such as spelling or multiplication, where slow thinking would be exhausting. The mistake is to confuse fluency with understanding and stop asking why. Varied practice forces students to transfer knowledge, which strengthens memory naturally. I believe the best method is to introduce ideas clearly, then revisit them through varied practice, ensuring that recall supports understanding instead of replacing it. Examinations should include unfamiliar problems so that pupils are rewarded for insight rather than rehearsed answers. Good teaching therefore combines clear explanation with repeated opportunities to use an idea in unfamiliar contexts and in conversation with other learners.", "understanding memorisation transfer rehearsal recall"),
])


# UNIT 7
UNITS.append([
    ("Building Healthy Daily Habits", "Write about creating sustainable healthy routines and their long-term benefits.", "请描述健康日常习惯的重要性，以及如何将这些习惯融入日常生活。", "Establishing healthy daily habits is fundamental to maintaining physical and mental wellbeing throughout life. Many people struggle because they attempt dramatic changes that prove impossible to sustain over time. However, research consistently shows that small, gradual adjustments produce far more reliable results than sudden lifestyle overhauls. For instance, beginning with a short morning walk can naturally evolve into a broader exercise routine within several months. Moreover, habits become automatic when they are connected to existing activities, such as stretching after brushing one's teeth. Consequently, the key lies in patience and consistency rather than perfection or intensity. It is also important to recognise that setbacks are entirely normal and should never be viewed as personal failures. On the other hand, rigid expectations often lead people to abandon their efforts altogether after a single missed day. A flexible attitude allows individuals to resume their routines quickly without unnecessary guilt or discouragement. Furthermore, tracking progress in a simple journal can provide valuable motivation and reveal patterns that might otherwise remain invisible. Ultimately, lasting health emerges from everyday choices repeated consistently, not from occasional bursts of extraordinary effort.", "Healthy daily habits are essential for lifelong physical and mental wellbeing. People often fail because they attempt dramatic but unsustainable changes. Research shows that small, gradual adjustments are far more reliable than sudden lifestyle overhauls. Habits become automatic when linked to existing activities, so patience and consistency matter more than perfection. Setbacks are normal and should not be seen as failures. Rigid expectations cause people to abandon efforts after one missed day, whereas a flexible attitude helps them resume quickly. Tracking progress provides motivation and reveals patterns. Ultimately, lasting health comes from everyday choices repeated consistently, not occasional intense effort.", "Good health is largely built through the routines we repeat every day rather than through occasional dramatic decisions. Many individuals set ambitious goals at the beginning of a new year, only to discover that such targets are difficult to maintain. In contrast, those who focus on modest improvements tend to achieve lasting results. For example, drinking an extra glass of water each morning may seem trivial, yet it can gradually encourage better nutritional choices throughout the day. Similarly, taking ten minutes to plan meals reduces impulsive decisions that undermine dietary intentions. Nevertheless, motivation inevitably fluctuates, and this is where self-compassion becomes valuable. People who criticise themselves harshly after a lapse are more likely to give up completely. Conversely, viewing a missed workout as a temporary interruption makes returning much easier. In addition, support from friends or family members can strengthen commitment and make the process more enjoyable. In summary, sustainable wellbeing depends on realistic expectations, consistent repetition, and a willingness to adapt when circumstances change.", "fundamental overhauls automatic setbacks consistency"),
    ("Mental Health Awareness at University", "Discuss why open conversations about mental health matter among students.", "请讨论在校园中公开谈论心理健康问题的重要性，以及学校可以采取哪些措施。", "Mental health deserves the same attention and respect as physical health, yet many people still treat it as a private weakness. Among university students, anxiety and depression have become increasingly common, often triggered by academic pressure and social isolation. However, silence tends to intensify these difficulties, because individuals suffering in secret rarely seek the support they need. Consequently, creating an environment where people can speak openly is essential for early intervention. Universities can organise awareness campaigns led by trained counsellors, which help to challenge widespread myths about psychological conditions. Moreover, peer support programmes allow students to connect with others who face similar struggles, reducing the sense of isolation. On the other hand, simply advertising services is insufficient if students fear that seeking help will damage their reputation. For this reason, confidentiality must be guaranteed and communicated clearly. Furthermore, teaching coping strategies such as mindfulness and time management equips students with practical tools. Ultimately, treating mental wellbeing as a shared responsibility benefits the entire academic community and prepares students for future challenges.", "Mental health requires as much attention as physical health, yet it is often treated as a private weakness. Student anxiety and depression are common, driven by academic pressure and isolation. Silence intensifies these problems because sufferers rarely seek help, so open conversation enables early intervention. Universities should organise counsellor-led awareness campaigns to challenge myths and create peer support programmes that reduce isolation. However, advertising services alone is insufficient when students fear reputational damage, so confidentiality must be guaranteed. Teaching coping strategies such as mindfulness also equips students with practical tools. Ultimately, mental wellbeing is a shared responsibility that benefits the whole community.", "Psychological wellbeing should never be regarded as less important than physical fitness. Unfortunately, many students believe that admitting anxiety is a sign of weakness and therefore remain silent. This attitude is harmful because untreated conditions gradually affect academic performance and personal relationships. Therefore, universities have a duty to make support visible and accessible. Regular workshops can teach students to recognise early warning signs in themselves and their friends. In addition, trained peer mentors provide a less intimidating first point of contact than professional counsellors. Nevertheless, institutional efforts will fail if the surrounding culture remains judgemental. Campaigns led by respected senior students can gradually change attitudes more effectively than official announcements. Moreover, flexible deadlines during periods of crisis demonstrate genuine understanding rather than empty sympathy. On balance, an open and supportive environment allows people to recover faster and develop resilience that will serve them throughout life.", "intensify intervention confidentiality mindfulness isolation"),
    ("Making Exercise a Lifelong Habit", "Explain the benefits of regular physical activity and how to stay motivated.", "请说明定期体育锻炼对身体和心理的益处，并提出保持运动动力的方法。", "Regular physical activity is one of the most reliable investments anyone can make in their long-term health. Scientific evidence consistently links exercise to a stronger cardiovascular system, better weight control, and a reduced risk of chronic diseases. However, the advantages extend well beyond the body, since physical movement also stimulates the release of chemicals that improve mood and reduce anxiety. Many people abandon exercise because they choose activities they genuinely dislike. Consequently, finding a form of movement that feels enjoyable is far more important than following a fashionable training programme. Moreover, exercising with a friend creates a sense of commitment that makes skipping sessions much less likely. On the other hand, overtraining can produce fatigue and injury, so rest days must be respected. It is advisable to set realistic goals and increase intensity gradually over several weeks. Furthermore, combining different activities prevents boredom and develops a wider range of physical skills. Ultimately, the best exercise routine is one that can be maintained consistently for years rather than weeks.", "Regular exercise is a dependable investment in long-term health, strengthening the heart, controlling weight, and lowering chronic disease risk. Its benefits are psychological as well, because movement releases chemicals that improve mood and reduce anxiety. People often quit because they select activities they dislike, so enjoyment matters more than following trends. Exercising with a friend increases commitment and reduces missed sessions. However, excessive training causes fatigue and injury, making rest days essential. Realistic goals and gradual progress are safer than sudden intensity. Combining activities prevents boredom and builds varied skills. Ultimately, the most effective routine is one sustained for years.", "Few habits influence wellbeing as profoundly as regular movement. Doctors frequently recommend exercise as a first response to mild depression, and the reason is straightforward: it changes brain chemistry in beneficial ways. Nevertheless, many people view physical training as a punishment for eating rather than as an enjoyable activity in itself. This mindset rarely survives beyond a few weeks. A more sustainable approach is to discover sports or pastimes that generate genuine pleasure, whether dancing, swimming, or simply walking through a park. In addition, social elements can transform exercise into something people anticipate rather than dread. Joining a local club provides both structure and companionship. On the other hand, enthusiasm must be balanced with patience, because injuries from excessive effort can undo months of progress. Consequently, beginners should increase duration slowly and include adequate recovery. To summarise, long-term fitness depends less on intensity than on consistency, enjoyment, and sensible pacing.", "cardiovascular chronic stimulates overtraining advisable"),
    ("The Principles of a Balanced Diet", "Describe what constitutes a healthy diet and how to resist unhealthy food trends.", "请描述均衡饮食的基本原则，并说明如何抵制不健康的饮食潮流。", "A balanced diet supplies the body with the full range of nutrients it requires to function effectively. This means consuming adequate protein, carbohydrates, healthy fats, vitamins, and minerals rather than focusing on a single food group. However, modern eating habits often undermine this principle, as processed foods high in sugar and salt are convenient and heavily advertised. Consequently, many people consume far more calories than they realise while missing essential nutrients. Moreover, fashionable diets promise rapid results but frequently eliminate entire categories of food without scientific justification. On the other hand, certain restrictions are medically necessary for individuals with allergies or specific conditions. The sensible approach is to read food labels carefully and prepare meals from fresh ingredients whenever possible. It also helps to eat slowly, because the body needs time to register fullness. Furthermore, cultural traditions often contain valuable wisdom about combining foods wisely. Cooking at home gives people greater control over ingredients and portion sizes. Ultimately, good nutrition is not about perfection but about making informed choices consistently over time.", "A balanced diet provides all the nutrients the body needs, including protein, carbohydrates, healthy fats, and vitamins. Processed foods high in sugar and salt are convenient but often displace essential nutrients, leading people to consume excess calories. Fashionable diets promise quick results yet frequently cut out whole food groups without evidence. Nevertheless, some restrictions are necessary for medical reasons. The best strategy is to read labels and cook with fresh ingredients. Eating slowly allows the body to recognise fullness, and cultural food traditions offer useful guidance. Ultimately, good nutrition depends on informed choices repeated consistently.", "Nutrition shapes nearly every aspect of physical performance and long-term wellbeing. A diet rich in varied natural foods supports the immune system, stabilises energy levels, and protects against chronic illness. Unfortunately, many consumers are confused by contradictory advice published online. Influencers frequently promote extreme eating plans that are neither affordable nor sustainable for ordinary families. In contrast, the principles of healthy eating are remarkably simple and have remained stable for decades. Meals should be based on vegetables, whole grains, and lean proteins, with treats enjoyed in moderation. Moreover, the social dimension of eating deserves attention, since shared meals encourage slower consumption and strengthen relationships. On the other hand, people with medical conditions require personalised guidance from qualified professionals rather than generic recommendations. Consequently, anyone experiencing persistent digestive problems should consult a doctor. In conclusion, ignoring marketing hype and relying on established nutritional principles leads to healthier and more-balanced eating habits.", "nutrients carbohydrates justification sensible ingredients"),
    ("The Importance of Quality Sleep", "Explain why sleep matters and how to improve sleep hygiene.", "请说明高质量睡眠对人体健康的重要性，并提出改善睡眠习惯的建议。", "Sleep is not a passive state of rest but an active process during which the body repairs tissues and consolidates memories. Adults generally require between seven and nine hours each night, although individual needs vary considerably. However, modern lifestyles frequently disrupt this natural rhythm through late-night screen use and irregular schedules. Consequently, millions of people suffer from chronic sleep deprivation without recognising its cumulative effects. Moreover, insufficient sleep impairs concentration, weakens the immune system, and increases the risk of accidents. On the other hand, simply spending more hours in bed does not guarantee better rest if the quality remains poor. Establishing a consistent bedtime is one of the most effective interventions available. It is also advisable to keep the bedroom cool, dark, and free from distracting devices. Furthermore, caffeine consumed late in the afternoon can remain in the bloodstream for many hours. A warm bath before bedtime can also signal to the body that rest is approaching. Ultimately, treating sleep as a priority rather than a luxury transforms both physical health and daily performance.", "Sleep is an active process of tissue repair and memory consolidation, not merely rest. Most adults need seven to nine hours, but modern habits such as late screen use disrupt natural rhythms. Sleep deprivation weakens immunity, reduces concentration, and raises accident risk, and longer hours in bed cannot fix poor-quality rest. A regular bedtime is highly effective, while a cool, dark room without devices also helps. Caffeine taken late persists for hours in the body. Ultimately, prioritising sleep improves both health and everyday performance.", "Rest has a far greater influence on wellbeing than most people acknowledge. During deep sleep the body releases growth hormones, strengthens immune responses, and clears waste products from the brain. Students and professionals alike frequently sacrifice these hours to meet deadlines, believing the extra time will improve their output. Yet research shows the opposite: tired workers make more mistakes and remember less of what they study. Fortunately, better sleep rarely requires dramatic lifestyle changes. Keeping the same wake-up time every day helps regulate the internal clock more effectively than sleeping in at weekends. In addition, exposure to daylight in the morning signals to the brain when night should begin. On the other hand, late meals and alcohol can fragment sleep without the sleeper noticing. Therefore, avoiding heavy food before bedtime is sensible. In short, protecting sleep is a practical strategy for boosting both cognitive performance and emotional stability.", "consolidates deprivation impairs interventions bloodstream"),
    ("Strategies for Managing Stress", "Discuss practical techniques for coping with everyday stress.", "请讨论日常生活中应对压力的有效方法，并说明长期压力可能带来的危害。", "Stress is a natural response that can sharpen focus in short bursts, but chronic activation of this system damages both mind and body. When pressures persist for months, people commonly experience headaches, disturbed sleep, and difficulty concentrating. However, the goal should not be to eliminate stress entirely, since some challenges are necessary for growth. Instead, individuals need to develop strategies that keep stress within manageable limits. Regular physical exercise is particularly effective because it burns off tension hormones and improves mood. Moreover, simple breathing techniques can calm the nervous system within minutes during an overwhelming moment. On the other hand, avoiding problems altogether usually makes anxiety worse over time. Consequently, breaking a large difficulty into smaller steps often restores a sense of control. It is equally important to maintain social connections, as talking with a trusted friend provides perspective. Furthermore, setting clear boundaries around work protects time for recovery. Keeping a simple diary of mood and workload can reveal which situations are most damaging. Ultimately, managing stress successfully means recognising early warning signs and responding before exhaustion sets in.", "Stress sharpens focus briefly but chronic stress harms mind and body, causing headaches, poor sleep, and poor concentration. Eliminating stress is neither possible nor desirable, so strategies should keep it manageable. Exercise reduces tension hormones and lifts mood, while deep breathing calms the nervous system quickly. Avoiding difficulties tends to increase anxiety, whereas dividing problems into steps restores control. Maintaining friendships offers perspective, and clear work boundaries protect recovery time. Ultimately, effective stress management depends on spotting warning signs early and acting before exhaustion develops.", "Pressure is an unavoidable feature of modern life, yet our response to it can be changed. Short periods of stress improve alertness, but prolonged exposure keeps the body in a damaging state of constant readiness. This condition undermines sleep, weakens resistance to illness, and makes rational judgement difficult. Therefore, everyone benefits from a personal toolkit of coping methods. Physical activity remains the most reliable option, as it processes stress hormones and releases mood-enhancing chemicals. Equally useful is the practice of pausing to breathe slowly when emotions threaten to overwhelm reason. Nevertheless, techniques that merely distract attention cannot solve underlying problems. On the contrary, tackling a difficult task directly often reduces the anxiety surrounding it. In addition, sharing concerns with empathetic listeners helps people see their situation more clearly. In conclusion, resilience grows when we combine immediate calming methods with long-term changes to our circumstances.", "chronic activation eliminate exhaustion boundaries"),
    ("The Value of Preventive Healthcare", "Explain why prevention matters more than treatment in modern medicine.", "请说明预防性医疗的重要性，以及个人应如何主动维护自身健康。", "Modern medicine has achieved remarkable success in treating diseases that were once fatal, yet prevention remains far more effective than cure. Preventive care includes regular check-ups, vaccinations, screening tests, and lifestyle advice designed to detect problems early. However, many people only consult a doctor after symptoms become severe and treatment becomes complicated. Consequently, conditions such as high blood pressure or diabetes often go unnoticed for years while causing silent damage. Moreover, early detection dramatically improves survival rates for several forms of cancer. On the other hand, excessive screening can produce unnecessary anxiety and costly follow-up procedures. Therefore, medical decisions should be based on individual risk factors and professional guidance. Individuals can take responsibility by knowing their family medical history and attending recommended appointments. Furthermore, maintaining a healthy weight and avoiding smoking reduce the likelihood of chronic illness considerably. Public health campaigns also play an important role in encouraging these habits across communities. Ultimately, a preventive approach shifts the focus from reacting to illness toward preserving wellbeing throughout life.", "Although modern medicine treats many diseases effectively, prevention is superior to cure. Preventive care involves check-ups, vaccinations, and screening that detect problems early. Many patients wait until symptoms are severe, allowing conditions like diabetes to cause hidden damage, while early detection greatly improves cancer survival. Nevertheless, excessive screening may cause anxiety and unnecessary procedures, so decisions should follow professional advice about personal risks. People can act responsibly by knowing their family history, attending appointments, keeping a healthy weight, and avoiding tobacco. Ultimately, prevention changes medicine from reacting to disease into protecting lifelong wellbeing.", "An effective health system must invest as much in preventing illness as in treating it. Chronic conditions develop gradually and often remain invisible until considerable harm has occurred. Routine examinations can reveal worrying changes long before patients notice any discomfort. For instance, measuring blood pressure regularly identifies a risk that would otherwise remain undetected for decades. In addition, vaccination programmes protect entire communities by reducing the spread of infectious disease. However, screening cannot be applied indiscriminately, because false alarms create distress and place pressure on medical resources. For this reason, doctors assess risk according to age, lifestyle, and hereditary factors. On an individual level, people should not wait passively for annual appointments. They can monitor their own habits, seek advice about worrying symptoms, and support family members in doing the same. In summary, the smartest medical strategy combines professional screening with personal responsibility for daily health choices.", "vaccinations screening unnoticed detection likelihood"),
    ("Achieving a Healthy Work-Life Balance", "Discuss the challenges of balancing professional duties with personal life.", "请讨论平衡工作与个人生活的挑战，并提出实现这种平衡的可行建议。", "Maintaining a healthy balance between professional responsibilities and personal life has become increasingly difficult in an era of constant connectivity. Many employees feel obliged to answer messages long after the working day has ended. However, this habit gradually erodes the boundaries that protect rest and family time. Consequently, people may achieve impressive career results while feeling exhausted and disconnected from those they love. Moreover, research suggests that overwork ultimately reduces productivity rather than increasing it. On the other hand, ambitious individuals genuinely enjoy their work and may not view long hours as a sacrifice. The key question is whether the arrangement remains a choice rather than an obligation. Practical solutions include setting fixed working hours and switching off notifications during evenings. Furthermore, employers can support balance by offering flexible schedules and respecting time off. It is also valuable to schedule enjoyable activities with the same seriousness as business meetings. Taking a proper lunch break away from the desk is a small but meaningful step. Ultimately, a sustainable career requires deliberate protection of the life outside work.", "Work-life balance is harder to achieve when technology keeps employees permanently available. Answering messages late erodes boundaries, producing exhaustion and strained relationships despite career success. Overwork eventually lowers productivity, although committed workers may freely choose long hours. What matters is whether the situation remains a choice. Practical steps include fixed working hours, disabling evening notifications, and employers offering flexible schedules that respect time off. Scheduling leisure as seriously as meetings also helps. Ultimately, a lasting career depends on deliberately defending personal life outside work.", "The boundary between employment and private life has become increasingly porous. Smartphones mean that a colleague can reach us at any hour, and many workers feel unable to refuse. This constant availability may appear impressive, but it gradually consumes the time needed for rest and relationships. In the long run, organisations also lose, because tired employees produce lower-quality work. Nevertheless, balance looks different for each person. Some people thrive on intense periods of work followed by complete breaks, while others prefer predictable daily routines. Therefore, rigid rules imposed from above rarely succeed. A better approach is to let employees negotiate arrangements that suit their circumstances and to judge them by results rather than hours logged. In addition, using holidays properly is essential for recovery. To conclude, genuine balance requires mutual trust between employer and employee, together with the courage to disconnect.", "connectivity obliged erodes sustainable deliberate"),
    ("Small Daily Choices for Better Health", "Explain how minor habits such as hydration affect overall wellbeing.", "请说明饮水等微小日常习惯对整体健康的影响，并讨论如何长期坚持。", "Major lifestyle changes receive most of the attention, yet small daily choices often matter just as much. Drinking enough water, for example, influences digestion, concentration, and body temperature regulation. However, many people forget to drink until they already feel thirsty, which indicates that dehydration has begun. Moreover, mild dehydration can cause headaches and fatigue that are wrongly attributed to stress. Consequently, keeping a bottle of water nearby is a simple and effective remedy. On the other hand, drinking excessive quantities brings no additional benefit and can be harmful. Similarly, brief pauses for stretching during long periods of sitting protect the back and improve circulation. These minor actions require almost no effort but accumulate into significant benefits over months. Furthermore, their simplicity makes them easy to maintain even during busy periods. It is important to remember that small habits cannot replace medical treatment when genuine illness occurs. Carrying a reusable bottle makes this advice far easier to follow at work. Ultimately, health is built from countless modest decisions repeated patiently rather than from occasional dramatic gestures.", "Small daily choices matter as much as major changes. Adequate water supports digestion, concentration, and temperature control, yet thirst signals that dehydration has started, which can cause headaches and fatigue. Keeping water nearby helps, although excessive amounts are unwise. Brief stretching breaks improve circulation and protect the back. Such simple actions demand little effort and accumulate into real benefits, making them easy to sustain. However, minor habits cannot substitute for medical care during illness. Ultimately, health results from countless modest decisions repeated patiently.", "People frequently underestimate the power of simple routines. Considering how much time we spend sitting and working, it is surprising how little attention we give to basic bodily needs. Water is an obvious example: even a small drop in hydration affects alertness and mood. Many workers rely on coffee instead, which may worsen the problem rather than solve it. In the same way, short walking breaks between tasks prevent stiffness and refresh the mind. None of these measures requires special equipment or unusual discipline. Nevertheless, their very simplicity can cause people to dismiss them. It is tempting to believe that only demanding programmes produce worthwhile results. In reality, modest habits survive difficult periods far better than ambitious plans. Of course, persistent symptoms require professional assessment rather than home remedies. Overall, good health grows through small, sensible actions that are repeated reliably every single day.", "dehydration circulation accumulate simplicity remedy"),
    ("Planning for Long-Term Wellbeing", "Discuss how to maintain health across different stages of life.", "请讨论如何在人生的不同阶段保持健康状况，并制定长期的健康计划。", "Health is not a fixed condition but a dynamic process that changes throughout life. Priorities differ markedly between adolescence, adulthood, and old age, so a rigid plan rarely works for decades. However, certain foundations remain valuable at every stage, including regular activity, balanced nutrition, and strong social connections. During youth, people often take their physical resilience for granted and neglect preventive habits. Consequently, damage accumulated early may appear many years later as chronic illness. Moreover, major life transitions such as parenthood or retirement can disrupt established routines. On the other hand, these transitions also create opportunities to reassess priorities and adopt healthier patterns. A flexible long-term strategy involves setting goals appropriate to each period of life. Furthermore, regular medical reviews help detect changes before they develop into serious problems. It is wise to build a support network that can adapt when circumstances change. Planning ahead makes these adjustments far less stressful. Ultimately, lasting wellbeing depends on the ability to adjust habits intelligently as the years pass.", "Health changes continuously, so priorities differ between youth, adulthood, and old age. Exercise, good nutrition, and social ties remain valuable throughout, yet young people often neglect prevention and suffer consequences decades later. Major transitions like retirement disrupt routines but also allow people to reconsider goals. Therefore, a flexible strategy should set age-appropriate targets, use regular check-ups to detect problems early, and build adaptable support networks. Ultimately, lasting wellbeing requires adjusting habits intelligently as life changes and circumstances evolve.", "Planning for health means accepting that our needs will change over time. A teenager and a retired person face completely different challenges, yet both benefit from similar basic principles. Regular movement, sensible eating, and meaningful relationships form a foundation that supports people at every age. Unfortunately, many young adults assume that good health will continue automatically and postpone preventive care. The consequences of this attitude often appear only in middle age, when habits are harder to change. Life events also reshape our priorities and available time. Having children may reduce opportunities for exercise, while retirement can bring welcome freedom. Rather than resisting these changes, it is better to adapt goals to the current stage. Periodic health checks provide useful information about emerging risks. In addition, maintaining friendships protects mental wellbeing during isolated periods. In summary, successful long-term planning combines stable foundations with flexible responses to changing circumstances.", "dynamic resilience transitions reassess adapt"),
])

# UNIT 8
UNITS.append([
    ("The Art of Active Listening", "Explain what active listening involves and why it improves relationships.", "请说明积极倾听的含义及其在改善人际关系中的作用。", "Listening is often confused with simply waiting for an opportunity to speak, but genuine listening requires far more attention. Active listening means focusing completely on the speaker and trying to understand both the words and the underlying emotions. However, most people interrupt mentally long before the other person has finished. Consequently, they respond to assumptions rather than to what was actually said. Moreover, offering immediate advice can make the speaker feel dismissed or misunderstood. On the other hand, silence without any response may seem cold and uninterested. A balanced approach involves asking clarifying questions and briefly summarising what has been heard. Furthermore, non-verbal signals such as eye contact and nodding demonstrate that attention is genuine. It is important to resist the temptation to judge or prepare a defence while listening. When people feel truly heard, they become more willing to share honestly. Paraphrasing the key points occasionally confirms that both people understand each other, and this simple habit prevents many unnecessary arguments. Ultimately, active listening transforms ordinary conversations into opportunities for trust and deeper understanding.", "Active listening means concentrating on the speaker and understanding both words and emotions, not merely waiting to talk. Most people mentally interrupt and respond to assumptions, while instant advice can feel dismissive. Yet silent indifference is equally unhelpful. The solution is to ask clarifying questions, summarise key points, and use eye contact and nodding to show attention. Listeners should avoid judging or preparing replies. When individuals feel heard, they share more openly. Ultimately, active listening builds trust and deeper understanding.", "Good communication begins long before we formulate a reply. It begins with the decision to give someone our full attention. Unfortunately, in a world of constant distraction, this gift has become rare. People often glance at their phones while a friend is speaking or plan their own response halfway through a sentence. Such behaviour communicates that the speaker is unimportant. In contrast, a listener who pauses and asks a thoughtful question makes the other person feel valued. It is worth remembering that listening does not mean agreeing. One can understand a position clearly while disagreeing with it. Nevertheless, empathy requires imagining how another person experiences a situation. This helps us respond with sensitivity rather than judgement. In addition, reflecting back what we have heard confirms understanding and prevents avoidable confusion. In conclusion, careful listening is an active skill that strengthens every relationship.", "underlying assumptions clarifying non-verbal temptation"),
    ("Resolving Conflicts Constructively", "Discuss how disagreements can be handled without damaging relationships.", "请讨论如何在不损害关系的前提下建设性地处理分歧与冲突。", "Conflict is an inevitable part of any close relationship, but its outcome depends largely on how people handle it. When disagreements are avoided indefinitely, resentment gradually accumulates and eventually damages trust. However, heated arguments driven by anger rarely resolve the underlying issue either. Consequently, the aim should be to discuss differences calmly and honestly rather than to win. Moreover, separating the person from the problem helps both parties feel respected. On the other hand, some conflicts reveal values that are genuinely incompatible, and compromise may be impossible. In such cases, accepting the disagreement respectfully is healthier than pretending agreement. Practical techniques include using statements that describe feelings without blaming the other person. Furthermore, taking a short break allows emotions to settle before discussion continues. It is essential to focus on the present issue rather than listing past grievances. Apologising sincerely for a genuine mistake can also unlock a constructive discussion that might otherwise never happen. Ultimately, constructive conflict resolution strengthens relationships by proving that differences can survive open conversation.", "Conflict is unavoidable, but avoidance breeds resentment while angry arguments solve little. The goal should be calm, honest discussion rather than victory, and separating people from problems preserves mutual respect. Some disagreements reflect incompatible values, so respectful acceptance may be better than false agreement. Useful strategies include describing feelings without blame, pausing when emotions rise, and addressing the present issue only. Choosing the right time to talk also prevents unnecessary escalation. Ultimately, handling conflict well proves that relationships can withstand honest differences.", "Disagreements need not destroy a friendship if both people approach them maturely. Suppressing complaints may seem peaceful, but unspoken frustration gradually poisons daily interactions. Equally damaging is the habit of attacking a person rather than addressing a specific behaviour. A constructive conversation starts with curiosity about why the other person sees things differently. By asking questions instead of making accusations, we create space for honest explanation. Moreover, acknowledging valid points in the opponent's argument lowers defensiveness dramatically and encourages cooperation. Nevertheless, not every dispute can be settled through compromise. When core principles conflict, the respectful response is to agree to disagree without hostility or resentment. Timing also matters, since discussions held late at night often escalate unnecessarily. Choosing a calm moment shows consideration for the other person. In summary, resolving conflict successfully requires honesty, patience, and a genuine willingness to preserve the relationship over winning.", "inevitable resentment incompatible compromise grievances"),
    ("Building Lasting Friendships", "Explain the qualities that help friendships survive over many years.", "请说明长久友谊所依赖的品质，以及成年人应如何结交新朋友。", "Friendships that last for decades are rarely accidental; they are maintained through deliberate effort and mutual understanding. Trust forms the foundation, because people need to feel safe sharing weaknesses and private concerns. However, trust develops slowly and can be destroyed quickly by dishonesty. Moreover, lasting friendships allow both individuals to change over time without losing their connection. Consequently, friends must remain curious about each other rather than relying on memories of shared youth. On the other hand, relationships require boundaries, since constant demands eventually produce exhaustion. It is healthy to accept that friendships naturally vary in intensity. Furthermore, practical support during difficult periods often matters more than frequent messages. For adults, meeting new people can feel challenging after leaving school or university. Joining clubs and volunteering create opportunities to meet others with similar interests. Although initiating contact requires effort, it often prevents the slow drift that ends many friendships. Keeping shared traditions alive also strengthens the sense of belonging. Ultimately, a good friendship combines reliability, honesty, and the freedom to grow independently.", "Enduring friendships depend on trust, honesty, and deliberate effort. Trust grows slowly but breaks quickly through dishonesty, and friends must accept that each person changes over time. Curiosity about one another matters more than nostalgia. Boundaries are necessary because endless demands cause exhaustion, and friendships naturally fluctuate in intensity. Practical help during hard times outweighs frequent messages. Adults can meet new friends through clubs and volunteering. Ultimately, strong friendships combine reliability with freedom to develop separately.", "True friendship is measured less by frequency of contact than by reliability when it matters. People may speak every day yet never reveal anything meaningful, while other friends remain close despite months of silence. The difference lies in trust. When we believe that someone will listen without judging, we become willing to expose our difficulties. Nevertheless, nobody can meet every emotional demand, and good friends respect limits. Expectations that never change create pressure as lives move in different directions. In addition, friendships should allow disagreement without threatening the relationship. Meeting companions becomes harder in adulthood, when work and family consume available time. Shared activities provide a natural setting for connections to develop. It is also valuable to initiate contact rather than always waiting for others. In conclusion, lasting friendship rests on honesty, acceptance of change, and small acts of support offered consistently.", "deliberate mutual dishonesty boundaries reliability"),
    ("Strengthening Family Relationships", "Discuss how family members can improve communication across generations.", "请讨论家庭成员之间如何改善沟通，尤其是不同代际之间的理解。", "Family relationships are often the longest and most complicated connections in a person's life. Unlike friendships, they cannot easily be abandoned when difficulties arise. However, closeness does not guarantee understanding, and generations frequently interpret the same situation differently. Consequently, parents and children may argue about choices that each side considers reasonable. Moreover, older relatives sometimes struggle to accept that younger family members have different values. On the other hand, younger people often dismiss advice that actually contains useful experience. Regular conversations during ordinary activities create opportunities for gradual understanding. Furthermore, showing respect for a relative's perspective does not require agreeing with it. It is important to listen without immediately correcting or lecturing. In addition, families should acknowledge achievements and express appreciation more openly. Family members can also set aside regular times for conversation without screens or distractions. Letters or voice messages help relatives who live far apart stay emotionally close. Ultimately, strong family bonds depend on patience, respect, and the willingness to see situations from another generation's viewpoint.", "Family ties last a lifetime and cannot be easily abandoned, yet closeness does not ensure understanding. Generations interpret events differently, causing arguments about reasonable choices. Older relatives may resist changing values, while younger members reject advice drawn from experience. Everyday conversations encourage gradual understanding, and respecting a view does not mean accepting it. Listening without lecturing and expressing appreciation openly strengthen bonds. Ultimately, good family relationships require patience and the ability to see things from another generation's perspective.", "Few relationships carry as much emotional weight as those within a family. Because relatives interact for decades, small misunderstandings can accumulate into lasting resentment. Communication improves when people recognise that criticism often hides concern. Parents who worry about a child's future may express themselves as commands, and the young person hears only control. On the other hand, adult children sometimes forget that their parents are adjusting to a changing world. Therefore, explaining decisions calmly can prevent unnecessary conflict. Shared meals and routine activities offer natural opportunities for conversation without pressure. In addition, forgiving minor mistakes is essential, since nobody communicates perfectly all the time. Nevertheless, healthy families also respect privacy and personal space. Regular telephone calls also matter when relatives live far apart, and small gestures of affection remain important. In summary, stronger family ties grow through empathy, clear expression, and realistic expectations of one another.", "complicated generations dismiss perspective appreciation"),
    ("Communicating Across Cultures", "Explain how cultural differences affect communication and how to overcome misunderstandings.", "请说明文化差异如何影响交际，并提出避免跨文化误解的方法。", "Communication involves far more than vocabulary and grammar, because meaning is shaped by cultural expectations. Gestures that seem harmless in one country may be offensive in another. However, most misunderstandings arise not from rudeness but from different assumptions about politeness. Consequently, a direct statement appreciated in one culture can appear aggressive elsewhere. Moreover, attitudes towards silence, punctuality, and personal space vary considerably. On the other hand, it would be a mistake to assume that every member of a nation behaves identically. Cultural knowledge provides useful guidance, but individuals must still be treated as individuals. Furthermore, showing genuine curiosity about local customs often matters more than perfect behaviour. When a mistake occurs, a sincere apology usually repairs the situation quickly. It is also helpful to ask respectful questions rather than making embarrassed guesses. Learning a few basic greetings shows respect and is usually appreciated warmly. Travel experience teaches this lesson faster than any textbook. Ultimately, successful cross-cultural communication requires humility, observation, and a willingness to learn from discomfort.", "Meaning depends on cultural expectations, not just words. Harmless gestures may offend, and direct speech can seem rude where indirectness is preferred. Beliefs about silence, time, and space also differ. However, individuals should not be judged by stereotypes of their nationality. Genuine curiosity about customs matters more than flawless conduct, and sincere apologies repair most mistakes. Observing how local people behave is often more reliable than memorising rules. Asking respectful questions beats guessing. Ultimately, cross-cultural communication demands humility and careful observation.", "When people from different backgrounds interact, trouble often begins with good intentions. A friendly gesture may carry a meaning nobody expected. In some societies, refusing an offer politely is normal, while in others it creates embarrassment. Such differences cannot be solved by memorising rules alone, because behaviour varies between regions and generations. A more reliable approach is to observe carefully and adapt. Showing that we respect local traditions usually matters more than getting every detail right. Moreover, humour is particularly risky, since jokes rarely survive translation. On the other hand, avoiding all contact for fear of mistakes prevents genuine connection. The solution is to enter conversations with patience and a sense of humour about ourselves. When confusion arises, calm questions are better than defensive arguments. This skill becomes invaluable in our increasingly connected world. In conclusion, cultural awareness grows through experience and honest reflection.", "offensive punctuality gestures humility discomfort"),
    ("The Power of Non-Verbal Communication", "Explain how body language influences the messages we send.", "请说明肢体语言等非语言交流方式如何影响信息传递。", "A large proportion of face-to-face communication occurs without spoken words. Facial expressions, posture, and gestures constantly signal our attitudes to others. However, people often focus so heavily on wording that they ignore these silent messages. Consequently, contradictory signals can undermine even the most convincing speech. Moreover, body language frequently reveals emotions that a speaker tries to conceal. On the other hand, interpreting gestures requires caution, because meanings differ between cultures and individuals. A crossed pair of arms may indicate defensiveness, or simply comfort in a cold room. Furthermore, matching another person's posture subtly can build rapport during a difficult conversation. Eye contact is particularly powerful, although too much of it may feel aggressive. It is important to ensure that non-verbal signals support rather than contradict the intended message. A person's tone of voice also carries information that words alone cannot express. Open gestures generally suggest honesty, though context must always be considered. Ultimately, awareness of body language allows us to communicate with greater clarity and empathy.", "Much face-to-face communication is silent, expressed through expressions, posture, and gestures. People concentrating on wording may send contradictory signals that weaken their message, and body language often exposes hidden feelings. However, gestures must be interpreted cautiously because cultural and personal meanings differ. Looking for several signals together is safer than judging one movement. Matching posture can create rapport, while eye contact is powerful but excessive staring feels threatening. Ideally, non-verbal signals should reinforce words. Ultimately, awareness of body language improves clarity and empathy.", "What we do often speaks louder than what we say. Before a conversation begins, others have already formed impressions from our posture and expression. A confident stance can make a hesitant voice sound more convincing, whereas closed gestures suggest discomfort. Nevertheless, reading body language is not an exact science. The same movement may express nerves in one person and boredom in another. For this reason, it is wiser to look for clusters of signals rather than isolated details. In professional settings, mirroring a speaker's movements gently can encourage openness. Yet copying someone too obviously appears dishonest and damages trust. In addition, cultural rules determine how much eye contact feels appropriate. Ultimately, the goal is not to manipulate impressions but to align our behaviour with our intentions. In short, genuine communication requires attention to both words and the body that delivers them.", "proportion undermine conceal rapport contradict"),
    ("Etiquette in Digital Communication", "Discuss the challenges of maintaining politeness in online messages.", "请讨论网络交流中保持礼貌所面临的挑战，并提出可行的建议。", "Digital messages have become a central part of relationships, yet they remove many of the cues that guide face-to-face conversation. Tone is particularly difficult to convey, so a brief reply may seem rude when no offence was intended. However, most people underestimate how easily written words can be misread. Consequently, disagreements that would end quickly in speech may continue for days online. Moreover, the permanent nature of messages means that careless comments can cause lasting damage. On the other hand, digital communication offers clear advantages, including time to think before responding. Furthermore, it allows shy individuals to express themselves more comfortably. It is advisable to read messages carefully before sending and to avoid emotional replies late at night. When a topic becomes sensitive, switching to a phone call often prevents confusion. Using clear subject lines and short paragraphs also makes messages easier to interpret. Emoji can help convey warmth, but they should never replace clear wording. Ultimately, polite online communication requires the same respect as conversation, together with greater awareness of missing context.", "Online messages lack the cues of face-to-face talk, so tone is easily misread and harmless replies may seem rude. Such misunderstandings last longer than spoken disagreements, and permanent comments can cause damage. Nevertheless, digital communication gives people time to think and helps shy individuals express themselves. Messages should be reviewed before sending, and emotional replies avoided at night. Sensitive topics are better discussed by phone. Ultimately, online politeness needs the same respect plus awareness of missing context.", "Written communication has changed relationships dramatically. A short message can reach somebody anywhere, but it lacks the warmth of a voice. Because readers cannot see our expression, they imagine one, and their imagination is often unkind. For this reason, important matters should rarely be handled through text alone. Nevertheless, writing has benefits that are easy to forget. It creates a record, allows careful formulation, and gives quieter people a fair chance to contribute. The trick is to choose the right channel for each message. Simple information suits email, while apologies or difficult feedback deserve a conversation. In addition, remembering basic courtesy makes a noticeable difference. Greetings and thanks cost little but signal respect. This is especially true when communicating with people from other cultures. To conclude, digital tools do not destroy good manners; they simply require us to apply them more thoughtfully.", "cues convey offence permanent advisable"),
    ("Empathy and Emotional Intelligence", "Explain why understanding emotions matters in relationships.", "请说明理解他人情绪的能力在人际关系中的重要性。", "Emotional intelligence refers to the ability to recognise and manage feelings, both our own and those of other people. In relationships, empathy is perhaps its most valuable component. However, many people confuse empathy with sympathy, although the two are quite different. Sympathy means feeling sorry for someone, whereas empathy involves imagining their experience from within. Consequently, a sympathetic comment may still create distance, while an empathetic response makes people feel understood. Moreover, empathy does not require fixing another person's problems. On the other hand, absorbing every emotion around us leads to exhaustion and poor judgement. Therefore, healthy empathy combines concern with appropriate boundaries. Furthermore, people with strong emotional awareness pause before reacting angrily. They can name what they feel and choose a constructive response. This skill is particularly valuable during disagreements, when emotions easily overwhelm reason. Labelling an emotion precisely often reduces its intensity and makes discussion easier. Curiosity about another person's reaction is more useful than immediate judgement. Ultimately, emotional intelligence allows relationships to survive difficulty because people feel safe expressing vulnerable feelings.", "Emotional intelligence means understanding and managing feelings in ourselves and others. Empathy differs from sympathy: it requires imagining another's experience rather than merely feeling sorry. Sympathetic remarks may create distance, while empathetic ones build understanding. Empathy does not mean solving problems, and absorbing others' emotions causes exhaustion, so boundaries matter. Emotionally aware people pause before reacting and name their feelings. Recognising our own mood also prevents unfair reactions to neutral comments. Ultimately, emotional intelligence helps relationships endure because people feel safe revealing vulnerability.", "Success in relationships depends less on clever conversation than on emotional awareness. Some people are highly articulate but remain unaware of the impact of their tone. Others say little yet instinctively sense when a friend is upset. The difference lies in attention to feelings. Empathy involves stepping outside our own concerns and considering another person's situation. Importantly, this does not mean taking responsibility for their happiness. Many helpers become exhausted because they forget this distinction. In addition, understanding our own emotions prevents us from projecting them onto others. When we feel irritable, we may interpret a neutral comment as criticism. Recognising our mood allows us to respond more fairly. Moreover, naming emotions openly can transform a tense atmosphere. This habit can be developed through patient reflection on daily experiences. In conclusion, emotional intelligence is a practical skill that improves every interaction.", "component sympathy absorbing appropriate vulnerable"),
    ("Developing Assertiveness in Relationships", "Explain how to express needs clearly without aggression or passivity.", "请说明如何在人际关系中清晰表达自己的需求，既不咄咄逼人也不过分退让。", "Assertiveness means expressing opinions and needs directly while respecting the rights of other people. Many individuals struggle to find this balance, alternating between complete silence and sudden aggression. However, passive behaviour rarely produces satisfaction, because unspoken needs remain unmet and others cannot guess what we want. Moreover, resentment often builds when people agree to requests they actually reject. On the other hand, aggressive communication damages relationships by ignoring others' feelings and imposing our will. Consequently, assertive language must be firm without becoming hostile. A useful technique is to state feelings and requests clearly, using calm and specific wording. Furthermore, it helps to distinguish between what is negotiable and what is essential before a discussion begins. When someone refuses a reasonable request, accepting the answer gracefully is also assertive. This clarity reduces misunderstandings and prevents quiet frustration. Everyone benefits when expectations are stated openly. It is important to remember that assertiveness is a skill developed through practice. Ultimately, honest self-expression strengthens relationships because others know where they stand.", "Assertiveness balances direct self-expression with respect for others. Passive silence leaves needs unmet and breeds resentment, while aggression harms relationships. Assertive language is firm but not hostile, using clear, specific wording. It helps to know which matters are negotiable and which are essential, and accepting a refusal gracefully is also assertive. Tone and timing strongly affect how requests are received. Clear expectations reduce misunderstandings and prevent quiet frustration from growing. The skill improves with practice. Ultimately, honest expression strengthens relationships because people understand each other's positions.", "Many conflicts grow because people hide their true feelings. They hope others will notice, but hints are easily missed. When frustration finally appears, it often emerges as anger that surprises everyone. A better approach is to communicate needs early and calmly. Being assertive does not mean winning every argument. It means stating a position honestly while allowing others the same freedom. Tone matters enormously here, since the same request can sound either reasonable or demanding. In addition, timing affects how messages are received. Discussing a complaint when someone is tired rarely succeeds. It is also useful to propose practical solutions rather than only identifying problems. Nevertheless, assertive people must accept that sometimes the answer will be no. This habit requires courage but becomes easier with repeated practice. In summary, clear and respectful expression prevents resentment and helps relationships develop honestly.", "assertiveness passive aggressive negotiable gracefully"),
    ("Maintaining Long-Distance Relationships", "Discuss how people can keep relationships strong when separated by distance.", "请讨论异地情况下如何维系亲密关系，并说明其中的困难与解决办法。", "Long-distance relationships place unusual pressure on communication, because physical closeness is replaced by deliberate effort and careful scheduling. However, distance does not automatically weaken a bond; neglect does. Consequently, partners must agree on how often they will communicate and what quality of contact they expect from each other. Moreover, short daily messages often maintain intimacy better than occasional lengthy calls, because they weave two separate lives together. On the other hand, excessive checking can create suspicion and resentment. It is important to trust the other person while remaining honest about insecurities. Furthermore, sharing ordinary details of daily life helps both partners feel included in decisions. When disagreements occur, waiting until a face-to-face meeting may be unrealistic. Therefore, difficult topics should be discussed calmly by video call rather than ignored. Visits require careful planning and sacrifice but provide essential emotional reinforcement. Patience is therefore just as important as affection. Ultimately, a long-distance relationship survives when both people invest consistently and keep a shared vision of the future.", "Distance itself does not destroy relationships; neglect does. Partners should agree on communication frequency and prefer brief daily messages over rare long calls, while avoiding constant checking that breeds suspicion. Trust must be combined with honesty about insecurities, and ordinary daily details keep both people included. Difficult topics should be discussed by video call rather than postponed indefinitely. Visits need planning but offer essential reassurance. Ultimately, long-distance relationships endure through consistent effort and a shared future vision.", "Separation tests a relationship in ways that proximity never does. When people live apart, they cannot rely on spontaneous moments together. Instead, they must schedule connection deliberately. Some couples find that planned video calls feel artificial at first. However, routine contact gradually creates a rhythm that both partners depend on. The content of messages matters less than their regularity. Silence for several days often causes more damage than a minor argument. Nevertheless, constant availability is not realistic, and expecting immediate replies creates tension. A healthy arrangement allows space for individual lives. In addition, discussing future plans keeps both people oriented toward the same goal. Shared hobbies can also make separate experiences feel connected. When visits finally happen, pressure to make every moment perfect can be disappointing. In conclusion, distance becomes manageable when trust, patience, and realistic expectations guide daily interaction.", "intimacy insecurities unrealistic reinforcement invest"),
])


# UNIT 9
UNITS.append([
    ("Why Saving Matters", "Explore the habits and rewards of regular saving.", "很多人觉得存钱枯燥又缓慢，请谈谈定期储蓄为什么重要，以及它如何影响一个人的长期生活。", "Regular saving is one of the most practical habits a young person can develop, yet it is often overlooked in school. To begin with, it creates a financial cushion that protects us when unexpected expenses appear, such as a broken laptop or a medical bill. Moreover, money set aside today can grow through compound interest, so patience is rewarded far more than impulse spending on things we soon forget. However, saving does not mean denying oneself every pleasure; rather, it means choosing deliberately between short-term comfort and long-term security. Consequently, people who save consistently often feel less anxious about the future and more confident about their decisions and their ability to cope with change. On the other hand, those who spend everything they earn may find themselves trapped in a cycle of debt and regret. In addition, a healthy savings habit gradually builds independence, because it reduces the need to borrow from family or friends. Ultimately, saving is not about greed but about respecting the value of one's own effort and securing genuine freedom.", "Saving regularly matters because it creates a safety net for unexpected costs and allows small amounts to grow steadily through compound interest over many years. It also trains people to choose long-term security over impulse spending, which reduces anxiety and builds confidence. Moreover, savings encourage independence by removing the need to borrow from relatives. Consequently, a person who saves consistently can face the future with greater freedom, resilience and respect for the value of their own daily effort.", "Although saving may appear slow and unexciting, it remains the foundation of a secure adult life. First of all, an emergency fund prevents a small problem from becoming a serious crisis, because there is always money available when appliances break or jobs are lost. Furthermore, regular deposits benefit from compound interest, which means that time is more valuable than large sums contributed occasionally. Admittedly, it can be frustrating to postpone purchases, particularly when friends are spending freely. Nevertheless, this discipline gradually changes one's attitude towards money and encourages better priorities. As a result, savers usually experience lower stress and greater confidence when planning their studies, careers or travels. Equally important, financial independence reduces pressure on families and allows young adults to make choices without shame or obligation. In short, the real reward of saving is not the amount collected but the peace of mind and autonomy it provides.", "cushion deliberately consistently genuine freedom"),
    ("Needs Versus Wants", "Learn to distinguish genuine needs from advertising-driven desires.", "广告常常让我们把想要的东西误认为必需品，请分析如何区分需要与欲望，以及这种区分对理性消费有什么帮助。", "Knowing the difference between needs and wants is essential for anyone who wishes to spend money wisely in a marketplace full of persuasive messages. Needs are the things that keep us healthy, safe and able to work, such as food, shelter and transport. Wants, by contrast, are the extras that promise comfort or status but are not necessary for a decent life. However, advertising deliberately blurs this boundary by presenting luxury items as symbols of identity and success and by linking them to happiness. Consequently, consumers often buy products they neither need nor truly enjoy, and regret follows once the excitement fades. Moreover, social media intensifies the problem because people constantly compare their possessions with those of others. On the other hand, a clear distinction between the two categories makes budgeting far easier and reduces impulsive decisions. Therefore, pausing to ask whether a purchase is necessary can save both money and emotional energy. Ultimately, mindful consumption allows us to spend on what genuinely improves our lives rather than on temporary impressions.", "Needs such as food, shelter and transport support health and work, while wants are optional extras linked to comfort or status. Advertising blurs this line by presenting luxury goods as symbols of identity, while social media encourages constant comparison. As a result, people buy things they do not enjoy and later regret them. Distinguishing the two makes budgeting easier, reduces impulsive decisions and saves emotional energy. Mindful consumption directs money towards what genuinely improves life rather than chasing temporary impressions.", "Many purchases that seem urgent are actually created by clever marketing rather than by real necessity. A genuine need is something without which daily life becomes difficult or unsafe, whereas a want merely makes life more pleasant for a short time. Nevertheless, advertisements are designed to attach strong emotions to ordinary objects, suggesting that buying a particular brand will bring confidence or friendship. In addition, peers and influencers make certain products appear normal, so refusing them can feel like social failure. For this reason, consumers should develop the habit of delaying non-essential purchases for a few days. During that pause, the appeal of most items disappears and the real priority becomes clear. Moreover, keeping a simple list of planned expenses helps people resist sudden offers in shops or online. Admittedly, this approach requires patience, but it gradually builds self-control. In the end, distinguishing needs from wants does not mean living without pleasure; it means choosing pleasures that last.", "persuasive luxury budgeting impulsive mindful"),
    ("Shopping Online Wisely", "Discuss the advantages and hidden risks of buying goods on the internet.", "网购方便又便宜，但也存在冲动消费和虚假宣传，请分析网购的利弊，并提出理性网购的方法。", "Online shopping has transformed the way people buy goods, offering convenience that traditional shops cannot match and a far wider choice. Above all, customers can compare prices within seconds and read reviews before deciding, which makes markets more transparent and saves shoppers both time and money. Moreover, home delivery saves time and allows people in remote areas to access products that were once unavailable. However, the same convenience encourages impulse buying, because a purchase requires only one click and payment feels almost invisible. Consequently, many consumers receive parcels they never really wanted and struggle to return them without losing money. In addition, attractive photographs and fake reviews can hide poor quality, so trust must be earned carefully. On the other hand, sensible shoppers can avoid these problems by setting a budget and reading independent feedback. Therefore, it is wise to wait before confirming orders and to check the seller's reputation. Ultimately, the internet is a useful tool when we remain alert and disciplined about what we truly need.", "Online shopping offers quick price comparison, customer reviews and home delivery, giving buyers transparency and access to remote markets. Yet one-click payment encourages impulse purchases, while edited photographs and fake reviews may conceal low quality. Sensible shoppers therefore set a budget, consult independent feedback, wait before confirming orders and check the seller's reputation. With alertness and discipline, the internet becomes a practical tool rather than a trap that drains our savings and creates lasting regret.", "Buying online brings undeniable benefits, but it also demands a higher level of self-control than shopping in person. The main advantage is efficiency: comparisons, discounts and delivery all happen without leaving home, and small businesses can reach customers far beyond their neighbourhood. Nevertheless, the absence of face-to-face contact weakens judgement, since colourful images and limited-time offers create a false sense of urgency. Moreover, dishonest sellers sometimes inflate ratings, so a product that looks excellent may disappoint on arrival. To shop responsibly, consumers should compare several websites and read critical reviews rather than only the glowing ones. They should also calculate the real cost, including shipping and possible returns, before paying. Equally useful is a waiting period of twenty-four hours for anything that was not planned. Consequently, the number of regretted purchases falls sharply. In short, online shopping rewards those who prepare carefully and punishes those who click without thinking.", "transparent impulse fake feedback disciplined"),
    ("How Advertising Shapes Us", "Examine the ways advertising influences choices and values.", "广告无处不在，有人说它提供信息，有人说它操纵欲望，请讨论广告对消费者选择的影响。", "Advertising surrounds us from the moment we open a phone to the moment we walk into a supermarket. Its most useful function is informational, because it tells people about new products, fair prices and services they might otherwise miss. Moreover, clever campaigns can support worthy causes and encourage healthier behaviour in the community and challenge harmful habits. However, much advertising does not simply inform; it creates desires by connecting products with beauty, success or belonging. Consequently, consumers may buy an item to express an identity rather than to satisfy a real need or genuine interest. On the other hand, repeated messages gradually shape what a society considers normal or desirable, especially among young people. Furthermore, emotional storytelling often weakens careful judgement, so viewers remember a feeling instead of facts. Therefore, developing media literacy is essential: we should ask who pays for a message and what it wants us to do. Ultimately, advertising is neither wholly harmful nor wholly innocent, and awareness is our best defence against manipulation.", "Advertising informs people about products and prices, and campaigns can promote good causes. Yet many adverts create desires by linking goods with beauty, success and belonging, so consumers buy identities rather than useful things. Repeated images shape social standards, particularly for the young, and emotional stories replace careful judgement. Media literacy helps us question who funds a message and what it asks us to do. Awareness, therefore, is the best protection against manipulation in daily life.", "There is nothing wrong with advertising in principle, since businesses must tell customers what they offer. In practice, however, the industry has become extremely skilled at selling emotions instead of information. A simple pair of trainers is presented as proof of confidence, and a breakfast cereal is linked to happiness and family warmth. Consequently, people judge products by the image attached to them rather than by their real quality. This is particularly worrying for teenagers, who are still forming their taste and self-respect. Moreover, social media influencers blur the line between honest recommendation and paid promotion. On the other hand, an informed consumer can enjoy advertisements critically and even learn from them. Comparing specifications, ignoring celebrity endorsements and recognising persuasive techniques all help. In conclusion, advertising reflects the values of a society, so education should teach young people to question it rather than simply reject it.", "informational desires literacy storytelling awareness"),
    ("Price and Real Value", "Reflect on why the cheapest option is not always the best.", "有人购物只看价格，请讨论价格与价值的区别，并说明为什么贵的东西不一定更好。", "Price and value are related, but they are never the same thing. Price is simply the amount of money a seller asks, whereas value is the usefulness and satisfaction a buyer gains over time. To begin with, a cheap jacket that tears after a month may cost far more than a durable one worn for years. Moreover, people often pay extra for a famous label, although the product itself may be identical to a cheaper alternative. However, this does not mean that expensive goods are always worthless; higher prices can reflect better materials, safer production and longer guarantees. Consequently, sensible consumers look at the cost per use rather than the number on the shelf. On the other hand, a low price can signal poor quality or hidden costs such as repairs and wasted time. In addition, emotional value matters too, since a handmade gift may be priceless to its owner. Therefore, asking what we truly receive in exchange is more useful than hunting for the lowest figure. Ultimately, real value lies in lasting benefit, not in temporary excitement.", "Price is what a seller charges, while value is the usefulness and satisfaction gained over time. A cheap item that breaks quickly may cost more than a durable one, and famous labels often add expense without improving quality. Yet higher prices can indicate better materials and guarantees, so money spent well is not wasted. Sensible buyers consider cost per use, hidden repair costs and even emotional meaning. Lasting benefit matters more than a low number on a shelf.", "Many shoppers assume that paying less is always a victory, but this simple rule can be misleading. The price tag shows only what must be paid today, not what an item will cost across its whole life. For instance, a poor-quality printer may require expensive ink and frequent repairs, whereas a reliable model saves money for years. Likewise, fashionable accessories with famous logos rarely perform better than ordinary ones. Nevertheless, it would be a mistake to dismiss all expensive products, because craftsmanship and safety are sometimes worth the extra charge. Consequently, the sensible question is not how much we pay but what we receive in return. Duration, usefulness and even personal meaning all contribute to genuine worth. On the other hand, a high price can also reflect clever marketing rather than quality. For this reason, comparing reviews and materials is more reliable than trusting a brand name. In the end, wise consumers measure value by lasting satisfaction.", "durable identical guarantees emotional value"),
    ("The Case for Simple Living", "Consider how owning less can improve quality of life.", "简约生活主张减少物品和欲望，请谈谈这种做法的好处，以及它可能面临的困难。", "Simple living asks people to own fewer things and to value experiences more than possessions. The main benefit is clarity: with fewer objects to manage, our homes become calmer and our attention is freed for relationships and ideas. Moreover, buying less reduces waste and lowers the pressure to earn money constantly. However, adopting this lifestyle is not as easy as it appears, because advertising and social expectations push us in the opposite direction. Consequently, people who declutter often feel relieved at first but later worry about appearing poor or different. On the other hand, simplicity does not mean rejecting comfort or living in an empty room; it means keeping what truly serves us. Furthermore, the extra time and money saved can be spent on travel, learning or helping others. In addition, a simpler routine makes it easier to notice what actually brings happiness. Therefore, the goal should be balance rather than extreme sacrifice. Ultimately, owning less can create space for a richer and more deliberate life.", "Simple living means owning fewer things and valuing experiences over possessions. It creates calmer homes, frees attention for relationships and reduces both waste and the pressure to earn continuously. The difficulties are real: advertising and social expectations encourage consumption, and people may fear seeming different or poor. Simplicity is not extreme sacrifice but keeping what genuinely serves us, so saved time and money can support travel, learning or generosity. Balance is the real aim of this lifestyle.", "The idea of living with less has become popular, and for good reason. When our shelves are crowded, our minds often feel crowded too, so clearing space can bring unexpected calm. In addition, fewer possessions mean fewer decisions, less cleaning and reduced spending on storage. Nevertheless, simplicity is harder in practice than in theory, because friends and colleagues may measure success by visible symbols. Moreover, online shops constantly suggest new products, making it difficult to feel satisfied with what we own. A useful approach is to keep items that are both useful and loved, while letting go of the rest gradually. Consequently, the process becomes a series of small choices rather than a dramatic revolution. Furthermore, the money saved can fund experiences that usually create deeper memories than objects. Admittedly, some people value collecting and decoration, and their choice deserves respect. Ultimately, the aim is not emptiness but freedom from unnecessary pressure.", "clarity declutter deliberate simplicity possessions"),
    ("Earning Your Own Money", "Discuss the value of financial independence for young adults.", "很多年轻人依靠父母生活，请谈谈经济独立的重要性，以及怎样才能逐步实现它。", "Financial independence is one of the clearest signs of growing into adult life. When people earn their own money, they can make decisions about study, work and lifestyle without asking for permission. Moreover, a regular income teaches the real cost of things, because every purchase comes from effort that could have been spent elsewhere. However, the first steps are often difficult, since young workers face low wages and insecure contracts. Consequently, some give up and return to the comfort of family support instead of facing the struggle. On the other hand, even a small part-time job builds confidence and a sense of responsibility. In addition, managing a limited budget encourages planning, which is a skill useful in every area of life. Furthermore, earning money changes relationships within a family, because gratitude replaces dependence and quiet resentment. Therefore, students should seek experience early, perhaps through holiday work or freelance tasks. Ultimately, independence is not about wealth but about the dignity of choices and responsibility for one's own path.", "Earning money lets young adults decide about study, work and lifestyle without permission, and it teaches the real cost of effort. Although first jobs may pay little, they build confidence, responsibility and planning skills. Success can be gradual: part-time or freelance work gives useful experience, and managing a small budget prepares people for larger challenges. Financial independence also improves family relationships by replacing dependence with gratitude. Its true reward is dignity and the freedom to choose.", "Depending on parents may be practical during study, but it delays the habits that adult life requires. The strongest argument for earning is freedom: money from one's own work allows choices about courses, housing and friends without constant explanation. Moreover, work teaches punctuality, patience with difficult people and the value of time, none of which can be learned from a textbook. Admittedly, young people often start with unfairly low pay, and the transition can feel discouraging. Nevertheless, small steps matter, such as tutoring, helping at weekends or selling unwanted possessions. In addition, a personal budget turns a limited income into a training ground for discipline. Consequently, responsibility grows naturally rather than through argument. Families also benefit, since admiration replaces the tension of endless requests. In conclusion, financial independence should be seen as a gradual journey towards self-respect, not as an overnight achievement.", "independence insecure contracts gratitude dignity"),
    ("Spending on Experiences", "Compare the lasting value of experiences and material goods.", "有人认为钱应该花在旅行和体验上，而不是买东西，请讨论这两种消费方式哪个更有价值。", "Many people now argue that money is better spent on experiences than on material possessions. Experiences, such as travel, concerts or learning a skill, tend to become part of our identity and memory. Moreover, they usually involve other people, which strengthens relationships long after the event is over. However, objects also have value, particularly when they are useful every day or carry deep personal meaning. A reliable bicycle, for instance, can improve health and save money for years. Consequently, the choice should depend on how long the satisfaction lasts rather than on fashion. On the other hand, experiences can be disappointing if they are planned only for photographs or social approval. Furthermore, not everyone can afford travel, so judging people by their holidays is unfair and rather snobbish indeed. In addition, a balanced approach often works best, combining a few lasting objects with shared activities. Therefore, we should ask which purchase will still matter in five years. Ultimately, value comes from meaning, not from the category of spending.", "Experiences such as travel or learning become part of our memory and often strengthen relationships, so many people consider them more rewarding than goods. Yet useful objects, like a reliable bicycle, can improve daily life for years, and a photograph-only trip may bring little joy. Fair judgement depends on lasting satisfaction, not on fashion or the price of holidays. A balanced mix of durable things and shared activities suits most budgets. Meaning, not category, determines real value.", "The fashion for spending on experiences rather than things is partly well founded. A journey or a course changes how we see the world, and the stories we bring back remain vivid for decades. Shared meals and trips also create bonds that objects cannot replace. Nevertheless, this preference can become another form of pressure, as people post holidays online to prove they live interesting lives. In reality, a cheap afternoon with friends may be more memorable than an expensive tour. Moreover, material things should not be dismissed: a good laptop supports study and work, and a family heirloom carries affection. Consequently, the wisest approach is to consider how often an item will be used and whether an activity will be remembered. On the other hand, experiences tied to status often disappear quickly from memory. In short, neither category is automatically superior; thoughtful choices create satisfaction, while careless ones waste money.", "possessions identity satisfaction balanced approach"),
    ("Borrowing and Debt", "Understand how credit and debt affect personal freedom.", "信用卡和贷款让消费更容易，请分析负债消费的隐患，并说明应如何避免陷入债务。", "Borrowing money can be useful, but it also moves pressure into the future. Credit cards and loans allow people to enjoy goods today while paying later, and this flexibility can help in genuine emergencies. Moreover, a mortgage makes owning a home possible for families who could never save the full price. However, easy credit encourages spending beyond one's income, especially when repayments seem small each month. Consequently, interest quietly turns a modest purchase into an expensive burden that grows with every delayed payment. On the other hand, debt reduces freedom, because a large part of future salary is already promised to someone else. Furthermore, financial stress damages health and relationships, and it can force people to accept jobs they dislike. Therefore, anyone considering a loan should calculate the total cost and check whether income is stable. In addition, building an emergency fund prevents borrowing when surprises occur, such as illness or a broken appliance. Ultimately, credit is a tool that serves careful users and traps careless ones.", "Credit cards and loans are helpful in emergencies and make large purchases such as homes possible, but easy borrowing tempts people to spend beyond their income. Interest increases the real price, and monthly repayments reduce future freedom. Debt also creates stress and may force unwanted career choices. To avoid it, borrowers should calculate total costs, check income stability and keep an emergency fund for unexpected bills. Credit rewards careful planning and punishes impulse and carelessness.", "Debt is not wrong in itself, but it should be entered with clear eyes. A student loan may open the door to a better career, and a mortgage spreads the cost of a home across many years. Nevertheless, small monthly repayments hide the true price, because interest continues to accumulate quietly. Consequently, people often discover too late that a convenient card has become a permanent obligation. Moreover, owing money limits choices: a person with heavy repayments cannot easily change job, travel or start a business. The practical remedy is simple but not always easy. First, borrowing should cover needs or investments, not impulse purchases. Second, the full repayment plan should be written down and checked against realistic income. Third, a small reserve of savings should be kept for emergencies. In addition, comparing different interest rates can save a surprising amount. Ultimately, financial freedom depends less on income than on controlling what we owe.", "mortgage repayments interest emergency burden"),
    ("Money and Happiness", "Examine whether wealth can buy happiness and lasting satisfaction.", "有人说钱买不到幸福，请讨论金钱与幸福感之间的关系，并说明什么因素更能带来满足。", "The relationship between money and happiness is more complicated than a simple slogan suggests. Money clearly matters because it provides food, shelter, healthcare and the freedom to choose how we spend our time. Consequently, people escaping poverty usually become much happier as their income rises and gain control over their daily lives. However, once basic needs are satisfied, extra wealth brings smaller and shorter benefits. Moreover, people quickly adapt to a higher salary and begin to desire more expensive things that once seemed unnecessary. On the other hand, comparing ourselves with richer neighbours can turn sufficient income into constant dissatisfaction. Furthermore, experiences that involve friendship, learning or helping others tend to create deeper satisfaction than private luxuries. In addition, good health, meaningful work and supportive relationships are closely linked to wellbeing. Therefore, money should be treated as a tool for building a good life rather than as the goal itself. Ultimately, wealth can remove obstacles to happiness but cannot create meaning on its own without personal effort.", "Money provides essentials and freedom, so income growth sharply improves wellbeing among people escaping poverty into security. Beyond that point, extra wealth gives shorter pleasure because people adapt to higher salaries and compare themselves with richer neighbours. Friendship, learning, helping others, meaningful work, supportive relationships and good health create deeper satisfaction than luxuries. Money should therefore be used as a tool for a good life, not treated as the final goal of human effort or the measure of success.", "It is true that money cannot guarantee happiness, yet it would be dishonest to deny its importance. Poverty limits choices, creates constant worry and often damages health, so a decent income is a foundation rather than a luxury. Nevertheless, researchers agree that after essential needs are met, additional earnings produce diminishing returns. One reason is adaptation: a larger flat or a new car soon feels ordinary, and the desire moves elsewhere. Another reason is comparison, since people measure their own lives against the possessions of friends and celebrities. By contrast, time spent with family, voluntary work and creative hobbies usually leave a lasting sense of purpose. Consequently, a sensible strategy is to earn enough for security and then invest time and attention in relationships. Admittedly, financial goals can themselves give direction and satisfaction. In conclusion, money removes many barriers to happiness, but meaning must be built from experiences and values.", "slogan escaping adapt sufficient wellbeing"),
])

# UNIT 10
UNITS.append([
    ("The Power of Imagination", "Discover why imagination matters beyond childhood and art.", "想象力常被视为孩子的游戏，请说明它在成人生活和解决问题中的作用。", "Imagination is far more than a childhood pastime or a talent for painting or writing stories. At its heart, it is the ability to picture possibilities that do not yet exist in the real world. Moreover, every invention, business plan and scientific theory begins as an image in someone's mind. However, many adults abandon this capacity because education rewards correct answers rather than original questions, and gradually lose confidence in their own ideas. Consequently, they approach problems with familiar methods and miss creative solutions that require a fresh viewpoint. On the other hand, imaginative people can test ideas safely in their heads before spending time or money. Furthermore, imagination helps us understand other perspectives, which makes communication and empathy stronger. In addition, it allows us to rehearse future situations and prepare emotionally for change and uncertainty in our private and working lives. Therefore, schools and workplaces should leave room for play, experiment and even apparently useless daydreaming. Ultimately, imagination turns uncertainty from a threat into an opportunity for growth and discovery.", "Imagination is the capacity to picture what does not yet exist, and it drives inventions, plans and theories. Many adults lose it because schools reward correct answers, so they rely on familiar methods and overlook creative solutions. Imaginative thinking lets people test ideas mentally, understand other viewpoints and prepare for change. Schools and workplaces should allow experimentation and daydreaming without fear of failure. It transforms uncertainty into a real opportunity for personal growth, empathy and shared discovery.", "People often treat imagination as a luxury, yet it is a practical skill needed in almost every profession. Engineers picture structures before drawing them, and doctors imagine how a treatment might affect a particular patient. Moreover, writers and artists rely on it to give shape to feelings that words alone cannot express. Unfortunately, routine can weaken this ability, because daily life rewards speed and repetition rather than exploration. As a result, we stop asking what could be different. Nevertheless, imagination can be trained: reading widely, travelling and talking to people from other backgrounds all feed it. Furthermore, allowing oneself to play with imperfect ideas often produces better results than waiting for certainty. Consequently, teams that welcome unusual suggestions solve problems faster. On the other hand, imagination without knowledge may remain beautiful but useless. In short, the combination of creative vision and careful testing is what turns a dream into a real improvement.", "pastime capacity daydreaming uncertainty empathy"),
    ("Curiosity as an Engine", "Explore how curiosity leads to discovery and learning.", "好奇心常被称为发明之母，请谈谈它为什么能推动学习与创新，以及如何保持好奇心。", "Curiosity is the quiet force that turns ordinary observation into discovery and fresh understanding. When we wonder why something happens, we begin to investigate instead of accepting the world as it is. Moreover, curious people ask questions that experts may consider naive, and such questions often open new paths. However, curiosity is easily discouraged by school systems that value speed and correct answers above genuine exploration. Consequently, children who once asked endless questions may become passive students who rarely wonder about anything. On the other hand, a curious mind keeps learning long after formal education ends, because the world itself remains interesting. Furthermore, it makes failures less painful, because each mistake becomes information rather than proof of inability. In addition, curiosity connects different subjects, allowing ideas from art to influence science and technology in surprising practical ways. Therefore, parents and teachers should protect questions rather than hurry towards answers. Ultimately, a society that values curiosity is better prepared for unexpected challenges and rapid technological change.", "Curiosity turns observation into discovery, because wondering about causes leads to investigation rather than acceptance. Curious people ask seemingly naive questions that open new paths and unexpected answers. Schools can weaken this habit by rewarding speed, yet curiosity continues learning after education ends and treats mistakes as useful information rather than shame. It also links subjects such as art and science. Parents and teachers should protect questions; curious societies handle unexpected change better than rigid ones.", "Children are born curious, but many lose this attitude as they grow older. The reason is not laziness; it is that school and work often reward quick, safe answers instead of exploration. When a question has no immediate use, it is pushed aside. Nevertheless, history shows that apparently useless questions frequently produce the greatest discoveries. Curious thinkers notice small details, compare unrelated facts and dare to ask why established rules exist. Moreover, curiosity makes learning enjoyable, so knowledge is absorbed naturally rather than forced. To keep it alive, we should read beyond our subject, travel with an open mind and speak with people whose interests differ from our own. In addition, we need the patience to follow a question even when the answer is unclear. Consequently, innovation becomes a habit rather than a rare event. In conclusion, curiosity is not childish; it is the engine of progress.", "observation investigate naive passive inability"),
    ("Creativity in Daily Life", "See how creative thinking improves ordinary routines and problems.", "创造力不只是艺术家的专利，请举例说明它如何改善日常生活，以及如何培养这种能力。", "Creativity is often mistaken for a rare gift, but in fact it belongs to everyone. At its simplest, it means finding a new and useful way to deal with a familiar situation. Moreover, creative thinking appears in ordinary acts, such as reorganising a small kitchen or explaining a difficult idea to a child. However, most people dismiss these moments because they do not produce a famous work of art or public recognition. Consequently, they wait for inspiration instead of practising the skill in small ways every day. On the other hand, creativity grows through repeated experiments and through borrowing ideas from unrelated fields. Furthermore, limitations can help rather than hinder it, because a tight budget forces us to invent simple solutions. In addition, being willing to fail is essential, since many good ideas begin as poor ones. Therefore, we should set aside time for unstructured play and notice problems that others ignore. Ultimately, creativity makes daily life more efficient, expressive and enjoyable for everyone involved.", "Creativity is a practical skill, not a rare gift: it means finding new and useful approaches to familiar problems. It shows up in ordinary acts, yet people often dismiss them and wait for inspiration. The ability grows through experiment, borrowing from other fields and accepting limits, which force simple inventions. Willingness to fail matters because good ideas often start poorly. Making time for unstructured play helps creativity improve daily life at work and at home.", "Many people believe that creativity belongs only to painters and musicians, but this view is far too narrow. In everyday life, creativity simply means noticing a problem and imagining a better response. A student who designs a clearer revision timetable is thinking creatively, just as a cook who replaces a missing ingredient is. Nevertheless, this kind of invention is rarely celebrated, so we underestimate our own ability. Moreover, fear of embarrassment stops people from suggesting unusual ideas in public. To develop creativity, it helps to expose oneself to different experiences and to ask what else an object could become. In addition, constraints should be welcomed, since limited time or money often produces the neatest solutions. Consequently, small experiments gradually build confidence. On the other hand, creativity without practical knowledge may remain only a nice idea. In the end, daily life rewards those who combine imagination with a willingness to try.", "reorganising inspiration hinder unstructured limitations"),
    ("Art as a Language", "Discuss how art expresses ideas that ordinary words cannot.", "艺术常被称为一种语言，请说明艺术如何表达难以言说的情感，以及它为何重要。", "Art allows people to communicate feelings that ordinary language cannot capture fully. A photograph, a song or a painting can suggest grief, hope or anger without a single spoken explanation. Moreover, art crosses borders, since an image can be understood by someone who does not speak the same language. However, some people dismiss art as decoration or entertainment without practical value, despite its deep influence on culture. Consequently, funding for music and painting is often cut when schools face budget cuts and pressure to improve test scores. On the other hand, creative expression helps individuals make sense of difficult experiences and build confidence. Furthermore, looking at art trains observation and encourages empathy, because we must imagine another person's viewpoint. In addition, public art can shape the identity of a city and give communities a shared symbol. Therefore, societies should treat artistic education as essential rather than optional, for children and adults alike. Ultimately, art matters because it records what people feel, not only what they know.", "Art expresses emotions that words cannot capture fully across different languages and cultures. Yet many people see it as mere decoration, so artistic funding is often reduced when budgets are tight. Creative expression helps people process hardship and gain confidence, while viewing art develops observation and empathy. Public artworks also give cities shared symbols and a distinctive identity. Art education deserves a secure place in every curriculum because art records human feeling as well as knowledge, imagination and shared memory.", "Some feelings resist precise description, and this is where art becomes essential. A piece of music can express sorrow more honestly than a long letter, and a photograph can freeze a moment that words would flatten. Moreover, artistic works travel easily between cultures because emotions are widely recognised. Nevertheless, it is common to hear that art is a luxury when practical subjects need attention. This argument overlooks the fact that creative activities teach patience, observation and the courage to experiment. Furthermore, making art gives people a safe way to explore painful memories. Even those who never become artists benefit from interpreting images and sounds. Consequently, museums and school concerts are not optional extras but parts of a full education. On the other hand, art should not be protected from criticism simply because it is beautiful. In short, its value lies in deepening our understanding of what it means to be human.", "capture dismiss empathy shared symbol"),
    ("Invention and Necessity", "Examine whether need or imagination drives new inventions.", "有人说需要是发明之母，也有人说想象力才是关键，请讨论两者在发明创造中的作用。", "The saying that necessity is the mother of invention contains real truth, but it is incomplete. Needs certainly push people to act, because a difficult problem creates pressure to find a practical answer to everyday difficulties. Moreover, limited resources often force inventors to reuse materials in clever ways that nobody had considered before. However, necessity alone does not produce a new machine or idea; someone must first imagine a different possibility. On the other hand, pure imagination without a clear need may remain an amusing toy that never improves anyone's life. Consequently, the most useful inventions usually join observation of a real problem with creative thinking. Furthermore, history shows that many breakthroughs were noticed only because someone questioned an accepted routine. In addition, persistence matters, since early designs rarely work perfectly, and several attempts may be needed before a useful version appears. Therefore, schools should teach both practical problem-solving and freedom to speculate. Ultimately, invention grows where curiosity meets genuine human need and patient experiment.", "Necessity creates pressure to solve everyday problems and inspire useful experiments, and scarce resources encourage inventive reuse. Yet need alone is not enough, since someone must imagine a different possibility; imagination without a real purpose stays a toy. Lasting inventions combine observation of genuine difficulties with creative thinking, a willingness to question routine and persistence through failed designs. Education should therefore join practical skills with free speculation about the future. Both forces are therefore essential.", "Most inventions begin with irritation: something is slow, expensive or inconvenient, and someone decides to improve it. In this sense, necessity provides both the question and the motivation. However, history also shows that many discoveries were made by people who were not looking for a solution at all. They noticed an unusual result and wondered whether it could be useful. Moreover, imagination supplies the courage to ignore conventional wisdom and try an unfamiliar method. Nevertheless, a clever idea that nobody needs will quickly be forgotten. Consequently, inventors must listen carefully to real users and observe daily frustrations. Furthermore, success usually follows many unsuccessful attempts, so patience is as important as insight. For this reason, education should combine technical training with open discussion and experiment. In conclusion, need points towards the problem, while imagination finds the path; progress requires both forces working together.", "necessity speculate persistence breakthroughs observation"),
    ("Technology and Human Values", "Consider how new technology should serve human needs.", "新技术层出不穷，请讨论技术进步是否总是改善生活，以及我们应如何确保科技服务于人的价值。", "Technological progress often improves life, but it does not automatically make society better. New tools can save time, reduce disease and connect people across great distances and give access to knowledge. Moreover, they create jobs and industries that did not exist before, raising living standards and expanding the range of choices open to ordinary citizens. However, every invention carries consequences that its creators may not foresee, and these effects may appear years later. Consequently, social media can spread information quickly while also damaging attention, privacy and honest debate. On the other hand, technology reflects the values of the people who design and use it. Furthermore, convenience should not be confused with happiness, since faster communication does not guarantee deeper relationships or lasting trust. In addition, unequal access means that some citizens benefit while others are left behind, which widens existing inequalities. Therefore, governments and companies must consider ethical questions before releasing powerful products. Ultimately, technology should be judged by how well it serves human dignity and freedom.", "Technology can save time, improve health and create new industries and global communication, but progress is not automatically beneficial. Every tool has unforeseen effects: social media spreads information while weakening attention and privacy. Technology also reflects human values, and convenience must not be mistaken for happiness or friendship. Unequal access deepens inequality between generations and regions. Therefore, governments and firms should examine ethical questions early, judging innovation by its service to human dignity and freedom.", "It is tempting to assume that every technical improvement is progress, yet history tells a more careful story. The same engine that carries food to hospitals can also pollute the air, and the network that connects families can spread rumours instantly. Moreover, new devices often arrive before society has agreed on rules for using them. Consequently, individuals must make difficult choices without guidance. Nevertheless, abandoning technology would be equally foolish, since it has eliminated diseases and reduced heavy labour. The real question is not whether to innovate but how to direct innovation. Companies should test products for fairness and safety, not only for profit. In addition, schools must teach young people to question digital information. Furthermore, access should be shared so that poor communities are not excluded. In conclusion, technology deserves admiration but also scrutiny, because its purpose is to serve people rather than replace human judgement.", "automatically foresee privacy ethical dignity"),
    ("Imagining the Future", "Think about how visions of tomorrow shape present decisions.", "人们对未来的想象会影响今天的选择，请讨论这种愿景的积极意义和可能的风险。", "Images of the future influence decisions long before that future actually arrives, shaping policies and personal plans. A society that imagines cleaner cities may invest in public transport today and schools may teach new skills. Moreover, personal ambitions are built on a picture of who we might become, which gives ordinary effort a sense of purpose. However, forecasts are often shaped by fear as much as by hope, especially during uncertain times. Consequently, pessimistic predictions can discourage investment and create unnecessary anxiety about jobs, health and security. On the other hand, overly cheerful visions hide difficult trade-offs and encourage careless optimism about miracle solutions. Furthermore, technological promises are sometimes used to sell products that do not deliver real change in people's daily lives. In addition, a single dominant vision can silence alternative ways of living that might be equally valid. Therefore, it is healthier to discuss several possible futures rather than treat one prediction as certain. Ultimately, imagination should guide preparation, not replace careful evidence about what is actually possible.", "Visions of tomorrow guide present choices, from investment in transport to personal ambition, giving ordinary effort meaning and direction. Yet forecasts often reflect fear or excessive optimism, which can cause anxiety or hide real trade-offs and costs. Companies may use bright promises to sell disappointing products, and one dominant vision can exclude other worthwhile lifestyles. Discussing several possible futures openly is much healthier. Imagination should guide our preparation while careful evidence keeps our hopes honest and realistic.", "A clear picture of tomorrow can be a powerful motivation. Communities that dream of safer streets will plant trees and redesign roads, while individuals who picture a useful career will study with greater purpose. Nevertheless, imagined futures are not predictions: they are wishes coloured by current fears. During difficult years, people tend to expect disaster, and this mood can prevent bold decisions. In contrast, advertising often presents a future of effortless happiness that no product can provide. Moreover, when one version of progress dominates public debate, other values may be ignored. For instance, a future built only around speed may neglect health and community. Consequently, the best approach is to compare several scenarios and ask who benefits from each. Furthermore, evidence should regularly correct our hopes. In short, the future is shaped by imagination, but responsible choices require both creativity and realism.", "forecasts pessimistic trade-offs dominant evidence"),
    ("Learning Through Play", "Argue for the value of play in developing creativity.", "游戏常被认为是浪费时间，请说明游戏对培养想象力和创造力的价值。", "Play is often dismissed as a waste of time, yet it is one of the most powerful ways of learning. When children invent games, they experiment with rules and imagine worlds beyond their experience and test impossible situations safely. Moreover, play allows failure without serious consequences, so mistakes become useful experiments rather than sources of shame. However, many parents now fill every free hour with organised activities and extra lessons designed to guarantee success. Consequently, children have fewer chances to invent their own entertainment or simply be bored without a screen or a schedule. On the other hand, unstructured play builds independence, because children must decide what to do without instructions. Furthermore, it encourages negotiation and empathy when several players must share an imaginary story. In addition, adults also benefit, since playful thinking helps them approach work more flexibly. Therefore, a balanced timetable should protect free time as carefully as formal study. Ultimately, play trains the imagination that later produces innovation in science, art and business.", "Play teaches children to experiment with rules and imagine new worlds beyond ordinary routine, and it makes failure safe. Yet busy schedules full of organised lessons leave little room for it, even though boredom can be creative. Unstructured play builds independence, negotiation and empathy through shared stories, while playful thinking helps adults work flexibly. Free time deserves protection alongside formal study. Imagination exercised in play often becomes innovation later in science, art, business and everyday problem-solving.", "Free play looks unproductive, but it is precisely this freedom that develops creative thought. When no adult provides instructions, children must invent goals, assign roles and solve disagreements themselves. Moreover, imaginary situations allow them to practise emotions and decisions without real danger. Unfortunately, modern timetables often replace this freedom with lessons, clubs and screens. As a result, many young people rarely experience long periods of boredom, which is often where original ideas begin. Nevertheless, play is not only for children: adults who joke, sketch or experiment casually often find solutions that formal analysis misses. Furthermore, teams that enjoy playful discussion share ideas more openly. Consequently, schools should keep break times and allow open-ended projects. Admittedly, learning also needs discipline and guidance from experienced teachers. In conclusion, play and instruction are partners, not enemies, and innovation depends on both in equal measure.", "unstructured negotiation imaginary flexibly innovation"),
    ("Borrowing Ideas Across Fields", "Show how ideas travel between art, science and business.", "创新常常来自不同领域的交叉，请说明跨领域借鉴为什么能产生新想法。", "Breakthroughs rarely stay within the boundaries of a single subject, even when that subject seems completely self-contained. Ideas from music, nature or architecture often reappear in completely different fields and solve problems in unexpected ways. Moreover, when people move between disciplines, they notice connections that specialists may overlook because they are too close to the issue. However, borrowing an idea is not the same as copying it; true innovation adapts and transforms the original. Consequently, artists who study science and engineers who study design frequently produce fresher work than those who never leave their own area. On the other hand, cross-field thinking requires humility, because each discipline has its own standards and methods. Furthermore, working with people from other backgrounds forces us to explain assumptions we usually take for granted. In addition, this exchange can challenge outdated habits and open new careers that did not exist before. Therefore, education should encourage broad interests alongside deep expertise. Ultimately, creativity flourishes where different ways of seeing meet and respect one another.", "Innovation often happens between subjects: patterns from nature, art or music can solve technical problems in surprisingly simple ways. Outsiders notice connections specialists miss, and adapting an idea is more creative than copying it. Cross-field work also demands humility, because each discipline has different standards, and it exposes assumptions we take for granted. Broad interests alongside deep expertise in one field therefore matter. Creativity grows where different perspectives meet, challenge one another and produce surprising combinations.", "Some of the most useful ideas come from people who refuse to stay in one lane. A biologist studying patterns of shells may help an architect design stronger roofs, and a musician may teach a programmer about rhythm and structure. Nevertheless, borrowing across fields is more than taking a surface image; it requires understanding why the original works. Moreover, specialists sometimes dismiss outside ideas too quickly, while outsiders may underestimate technical difficulties. Consequently, the best results come from genuine collaboration rather than casual imitation. Furthermore, explaining one's assumptions to a partner from another discipline often reveals weaknesses in familiar methods. In addition, such projects prepare students for a world where problems rarely arrive labelled by subject. Admittedly, deep knowledge still matters and cannot be replaced by curiosity alone. In conclusion, innovation thrives when expertise is combined with the courage to look elsewhere.", "breakthroughs disciplines overlook humility expertise"),
    ("Imagination and Empathy", "Explore how imagining other lives builds understanding and kindness.", "想象力能帮助我们理解他人，请说明这种能力如何促进同理心，并谈谈它对社会的意义。", "To understand another person, we must first imagine what their life feels like. Imagination allows us to step outside our own experience and see a situation through different eyes, especially when their background differs sharply from ours. Moreover, stories and films train this ability by asking us to identify with characters unlike ourselves and feel their hopes and fears. However, imagination alone is not enough, because it can also create stereotypes if we never question our assumptions. Consequently, real empathy requires both creative insight and genuine listening. On the other hand, people who can picture another's difficulties are more likely to offer practical help. Furthermore, this skill reduces conflict, since it becomes harder to dismiss opinions we have tried to understand. In addition, societies with strong imaginative cultures often support art, literature and education for everyone, not just for a privileged few. Therefore, schools should use stories and discussion to develop this capacity in every student. Ultimately, an imaginative society is not only cleverer but also kinder.", "Understanding others begins with imagining their experience, and stories help us identify with people unlike ourselves in distant places. Yet unchecked imagination can produce stereotypes, so empathy also needs listening and evidence. People who picture another's hardship offer more help, and understanding opposing views reduces conflict. Imaginative societies value art and education widely, across all social groups. Schools can develop this capacity through stories and discussion, producing cleverer and kinder citizens than narrow instruction alone.", "Empathy is often described as a feeling, but it also requires imagination. Without the ability to picture another person's situation, we remain trapped inside our own concerns. Literature is valuable here because it invites us to live temporarily in a different mind. Nevertheless, this imagined understanding can be misleading if it relies on convenient stereotypes rather than real information. Moreover, people sometimes feel sympathetic in theory but behave selfishly when help is actually needed. Consequently, true empathy should lead to practical action, such as listening patiently or changing an unfair rule. Furthermore, it improves public discussion, because opponents become human beings rather than caricatures. In addition, a community that encourages imagination through art and conversation tends to include more voices. Admittedly, empathy has limits and cannot solve every disagreement. In conclusion, imagination widens our moral attention, making society both more tolerant and more cooperative.", "stereotypes genuine listening capacity imaginative"),
])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    with open(os.path.join(CONTENT_DIR, "b2-writing.md"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(build_course_file())
    print("wrote b2-writing.md")

    problems = []
    for i, (theme, icon, color) in enumerate(PALETTE, start=1):
        lessons = UNITS[i - 1]
        if len(lessons) != 10:
            problems.append(f"Unit {i} ({theme}) has {len(lessons)} lessons, expected 10")
        text = build_unit_file(i, theme, icon, color, lessons)
        with open(os.path.join(CONTENT_DIR, f"b2-writing-unit-{i}.md"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(text)
        for (title, _desc, _zh, answer, core, acceptAlso, _srs) in lessons:
            for label, val, lo, hi in [
                ("answer", answer, 150, 200),
                ("acceptAlso", acceptAlso, 120, 200),
                ("core", core, 70, 110),
            ]:
                n = wc(val)
                if n < lo or n > hi:
                    problems.append(f"Unit {i} '{title}': {label} {n} words (want {lo}-{hi})")
    print("wrote 10 unit files")
    if problems:
        print("PROBLEMS:")
        for p in problems:
            print("  -", p)
        raise SystemExit(1)
    print("ALL WORD COUNTS OK (answer 150-200, acceptAlso 120-200, core 70-110)")
